Members of the Wales Assembly are worried about 'making clown figures'
Consternation is perceived among some members of the Assembly in front of the proposal to change their title to MWP (Member of the Welsh Parliament (Member of the Welsh Parliament).
This proposal arose as a result of the programmes to change the name of the Assembly in the Parliament of Wales.
Members of the Assembly of the entire political arena are concerned that this might sound ridiculous.
A member of the Assembly of the Labour Party has said that his party is worried that “he can rhyme with TWP and PWP.”
For readers who don't live in Wales: In Welsh, twp means cretino and pwp means poop.
A member of the Plaid Assembly said that the group overall “wasn’t happy” and suggested alternatives.
A member of the Welsh Conservative Party said he was “opened” towards changing the name, but pointed out that only a small verbal step separates MWP from Muppet (clown).
In this context, the letter W in Welsh is pronounced in a similar way to Yorkshire's English pronunciation of the letter U.
The assembly committee, which is currently being drafted for the introduction of name changes, said: "The final decision on any descriptors of how the members of the Assembly will be called will of course be a topic of debate for the members themselves."
The Law of the Government of Wales of 2017 granted the Welsh assembly the right to change its name.
In June, the Commission published the results of a public consultation on the proposals, which registered broad support for the possibility of calling the Parliament of Wales Assembly.
With regard to the title of members of the Assembly, the Commission expressed its preference in favour of Welsh Parliament Members (Welsh Parliament Members), but the MWP option received greater consent in a public consultation.
AMs are apparently proposing alternative options, but difficulties to reach consensus could prove to be a headache for President Elin Jones, who is expected to submit a legislative proposal regarding the changes within weeks.
The Reforms Act will include further changes to the functioning of the assembly, including regulations on the exclusion of AMs and the design of the committee system.
AMs will get the final vote on their name during the discussion of the legislation.
The Macedonians go to the polls for the referendum on the modification of the name of the country
On Sunday voters will decide whether to change their country’s name to “Republic of North Macedonia.”
The popular vote was organized in an attempt to end an ongoing dispute for decades with neighboring Greece, which owns a province called Macedonia.
Athens has long stated that the name of its northern neighbour represents a vindication over its territories and has repeatedly opposed its applications for membership of the EU and NATO.
The President of Macedonia Gjorge Ivanov, an opponent of the plebiscite for the modification of the name, has said that he will not take into account the outcome of the vote.
However, supporters of the referendum, including Prime Minister Zoran Zaev, say the name change is simply the price to be able to join the EU and NATO.
St. Martin's bells shut up while Harlem's churches are struggling
“The elders I spoke to say that in the past there was a bar and a church on every corner,” Adams said.
“There’s neither of us today.”
He explained that the disappearance of bars was understandable.
“People socialize differently today,” he explained.
"Bars are no longer the neighborhood living rooms that people frequent regularly."
As for the churches, he worries that the money from selling assets won’t last as long as leaders expect, “and sooner or later they’ll go back where they started.”
The churches, he added, could be replaced by apartment buildings with condominiums filled with the type of people who won't help the neighborhood's remaining sanctuaries.
“The vast majority of the people who buy apartments in these buildings are white,” he says, “and for that reason they’ll hasten to the time when these churches will shut down altogether because it’s unlikely that most of the people moving into these apartments will become a member of these churches.”
Both churches were built by fraternities before Harlem became a black neighborhood - Metropolitan Community in 1870 and St Martint's a decade later.
The initial White Methodist congregation left in the 1930s.
A black congregation praying in a nearby church took possession of the building.
St. Martin's was acquired by a black congregation headed by Reverend John Howard Johnson, who led the boycot against retailers of the 125th Street, one of the main shopping streets in Harlem, who denied hiring or promoting black people.
Fire in 1939 severely damaged the building, but while the parishioners of Father Johnson planned its reconstruction, they also ordered the music box.
Reverend David Johnson, son of Father Johnson and his successor at St. Martin’s, proudly called the carillon “the bells of the poor.”
The expert who played the carillon in July called it in a different way: "A cultural treasure" and "an irreplacable historical instrument."
The expert, Tiffany Ng of the University of Michigan, also pointed out that it was the world's first music box to be played by a black musician, Dionisio A. Lind, who switched to a larger music box at Riverside church 18 years ago.
Merriweather claimed that St. Martin's didn't replace him.
What has been staged at St. Martin's in recent months has been a complicated plot of architects and entrepreneurs, some presented by lay church leaders, others by the episcopal diocese.
The parish assembly, the governing body of the parish, constituted by lay leaders, wrote to the diocese in July about its concerns that the diocese "tried to unload the costs" on the parish assembly although the latter was not involved in the hiring of architects and entrepreneurs sent by the diocese.
Some parishioners complained about a lack of transparency from the diocese.
Shark injures a thirteen-year-old diving lobster fisher in California
Official sources declare that on Saturday a shark attacked and injured a 13-year-old boy while he was diving in search of lobsters in California, during the opening day of the lobster season.
The attack occurred just before 7 a.m. near Beacon's Beach in Encinitas.
Chad Hammel told KSWB-TV in San Diego that for half an hour he had been diving with friends on Saturday morning when he heard a boy shout and swam up to him with a group of people to get him out of the water.
Hammel thought at the beginning that it was only enthusiasm due to the capture of a lobster but then “he understood that the boy was shouting ‘I’ve been bitten!
I've been bitten!"
His entire collarbone was torn,” he said, noticing Hammel once he reached the boy.
"I shouted to everyone to get out of the water: 'There's a shark in the water!'" Hammel added.
The boy was transported by helicopter to Rady Children's Hospital in San Diego, where he was hospitalized in critical condition.
The shark species responsible for the attack is not known.
The beachager Captain Larry Giles stated during a communication to the media that a few weeks earlier a shark had been spotted in the area, but that it had been declared not belonging to any dangerous species.
Giles added that the victim suffered traumatic injuries in the upper area of the chest.
Authorities banned access to the beach from Ponto Beach in Casablad to Swami's to Ecinitas for 48 hours for investigative and security purposes.
Giles has announced that there are more than 135 species of sharks in the area, but that most are not considered dangerous.
Sainsbury's programme a boost towards the beauty products industry in the UK
Sainsbury's is challenging Boots, Superdrug and Debenhams with lanes dedicated to department store beauty products assigned to specialist assistants
As part of the UK’s £2.8bn beauty products market, which continues to grow as sales in the fashion and house goods sectors decrease, the largest lanes dedicated to beauty products will be tested in 11 stores in the country and adopted in multiple stores next year if successful.
The investment in beauty takes place at a time when supermarkets are on the hunt for new ways to use the space on shelves when dedicated to televisions, microwave ovens and household items.
Sainsbury's said it would double the size of its beauty item offering to reach 3,000 products, including for the first time brands like Revlon, Essie, Tweezerman and Dr. PawPaw.
L’Oreal, Maybelline and Burt’s Bees’ existing ranges will occupy a larger space with branded areas similar to those in stores like Boots.
The supermarket is also relaunching its range of Boutique tricks so that most products are vegan - a quality increasingly in demand from younger buyers.
In addition, perfume retailer The Fragrance Shop will experience permissions in two Sainsbury's stores, the first of which opened in Croydon, South London while a second will open in Selly Oak, Birmingham, later over the year.
Online shopping and a shift towards buying small amounts of groceries daily at small neighborhood stores have forced supermarkets to commit more to getting people to buy from them.
Mike Coupe, chief executive of Sainsbury's, said the outlets will increasingly resemble department stores as the supermarket chain seeks to resist discounters like Aldi and Lidl by proposing more non-food services and products.
Sainsbury's has introduced Argos outlets to hundreds of stores and has also introduced several Habitats since it bought the two chains about two years ago, and such a strategy boosted food sales and made acquisitions more profitable.
The supermarket's previous attempt to restore enamel to its departments of beauty products and pharmaceuticals ended in a failure.
Sainsbury's tried a joint venture with Boots in the early 2000s but the synergy ended in a dispute over splitting revenue from pharmaceutical outlets in supermarkets.
The new strategy was adopted after Sainsbury's sold three years ago its pharmaceutical business of 281 outlets in Celesio, the owner of the Lloyds Pharmacy chain, for 125 million pounds.
Lloyds was expected to play a role in the programme, with the addition in four stores of an extensive range of luxury brands of skincare products, including La Roche-Posay and Vichy.
Paul Mills-Hicks, Sainsbury’s Commercial Director, said: “We’ve transformed the look of our beauty product departments to improve the environment for our customers.
We have also invested in specially trained colleagues who will be available to offer advice.
Our range of brands has been developed to meet every need and the inviting environment and the comfortable locations make us an attractive destination for beauty products, in contrast to the old way of shopping.”
Peter Jones 'furious after Holly Willoughby parades from 11-million-pound deal
Draons Den star Peter Jones is furious after TV presenter Holly Willoughby withdrew from an €11m contract with his trending brand to focus on his new contracts with Marks and Spencer and ITV
Willoughby no longer has time for its brand of Truly housewives and accessories.
The couple's business had been compared to Gwyneth Paltrow's Goop brand.
This Morning presenter, 37, appealed to Instagram to announce its abandonment.
Holly Willoughby has left the star of Dragons Den, Peter Jones, furious after retiring from their lucrative last-minute trendy business to focus on his exceptional new contracts with Marks & Spencer and ITV.
Some sources claim that Jones was “furious” when TV’s golden girl admitted during a tense meeting at her business empire’s headquarters in Marlow, Buckinghamshire, that her new contracts – worth 1.5 million pounds – didn’t leave her enough time to dedicate themselves to their brand of housewives and Truly accessories.
The activity was compared to Gwyneth Paltrow's Goop's Goop brand and it had been estimated to double Willoughby's assets, estimated at around £11m.
As soon as Willoughby, 37, chose Instagram to announce his abandonment of Truly, Jones left Britain to head to one of his holiday residencies.
“Truly was undoubtedly Holly’s top priority.
It was supposed to be her long-term future that would engage her for the next twenty years.
His decision to quit has left all those involved absolutely speechless.
No one could believe what was happening on Tuesday, they were so close to launch.
“There’s a warehouse full of products at Marlow’s headquarters, ready to be sold.”
Experts believe the abandonment of the presenter of This Morning, one of the UK's most profitable stars, could cost the company millions because of strong investments in products ranging from cushions and candies to clothing and homewear and due to possible delays at brand launch.
And it could also mean the end of a long friendship.
Willoughby, mother of three, and her husband Dan Baldwin have been friends with Jones and his wife Tara Capp for ten years.
Willoughby devised Truly with Capp in 2016 and Jones, 52, joined the company as president in March.
The couples spent their holidays together and Jones owns a 40 percent stake in Baldwin's television production company.
Willoughby will become the ambassador of the M&S brand and will replace Ant McPartlin as host of ITV’s “I’m A Celebrity” program.
A source close to Jones claimed last night that “he doesn’t wish to comment on his commercial affairs.”
Hard words and “then we fell in love”
He joked about the criticism he allegedly received from the media to make a comment that some would judge "non-presidential" and to be so positive about the North Korean leader.
Why did President Trump give up so much?
Trump said in his voice in imitation of the "news host."
“I didn’t give up anything.”
He stressed that Kim is interested in a second meeting after the first occurred in Singapore in June was cheered by Trump as a huge step towards the denuclearization of North Korea.
But denuclearisation negotiations are at a standstill.
More than three months after the June summit in Singapore, North Korean diplomat Ri Yong Ho addressed world leaders during the U.N. General Assembly on Saturday that North Korea did not observe an “equivalent response” from the U.S
Instead, he stressed, the U.S. is continuing to impose sanctions aimed at keeping pressure high.
Trump gave a far more optimistic view of this speech.
