While Save the Children is organizing a team that will travel to Palu, it's still unsure that foreign staff will be able to work on the ground.
Sutopo, a spokeswoman for the National Calamity Agency, said the Indonesian authorities were assessing the situation in Palu to decide whether international agencies would be allowed to contribute aid.
Considering the earthquakes to which Indonesia is continuously subjected, the country continues to be terribly unprepared in the face of the fury of nature.
Although refuges have been built for tsunamis in Aceh, it is not a common structure on other coasts.
The obvious lack of a tsunami warning siren in Palu, although the alarm had been given, has probably contributed to the loss of lives.
In the best conditions, moving between the many islands of Indonesia is complicated.
Natural calamities make logistics even more complicated.
A hospital ship anchored in Lombok to assist the victims of the quake is heading towards Palu but it will take at least three days to reach the site of the new calamity.
President Joko Widodo has put at the center of his own election campaign the improvement of infrastructure in shreds of Indonesia and poured money on roads and railways.
But lacks of funds hit Joko's administration as he prepares for re-election next year.
Joko also faces pressure from persistent sectarian tensions in Indonesia, where members of the Muslim majority have adopted the most conservative branch of the religion.
Over 1,000 people were killed and tens of thousands turned away from their homes due to clashes on the streets between Christian and Muslim gangs, which wrestled using machetes, bows and arrows and other rudimentary weapons.
Watch: Liverpool's Daniel Sturridge draws against Chelsea
Daniel Sturridge saved Liverpool from a Premier League loss to Chelsea with a point in the 89th minute on Saturday, at Stamford Bridge in London.
Sturridge received a pass from Xherdan Shaqiri as he stood about 25 yards from Chelsea's goal, with his team down 1-0.
He touched the left ball before pulling on the second post.
The attempt passed high above the frame of the gate as it veered towards the upper-right corner of the net.
The ball finally fell behind Kepa Arrizabalaga bagging into the net.
"It was enough to try to get in position, to get to the ball and players like Shaq always play forward as far as I can, so I've been trying to create myself as much time as possible," Sturridge told LiverpoolFC.com.
"I saw Kante coming in and gave it a touch and I didn't think too much about it and I pulled."
Chelsea won 1-0 in the first half after the 25th-minute goal scored by Belgium star Eden Hazard.
The centre-forward of the Blue switched heels to Mateo Kovacic before splashing away near the half-court and snapping in the middle of Liverpool.
Kovacic made a quick half-court pass.
So he performed a beautiful ride that took Hazard into the area.
Hazard overcame the defence and finished on the second with a left-footed goal beyond Alisson Becker of Liverpool.
Liverpool will face Napoli in the Champions League group stage at 15.00 on Wednesday at the San Paolo Stadium in Naple, Italy.
Chelsea will face Videoton in the UEFA Europa League at 15.00 on Thursday in London.
Indonesia's Tsunain death toll rises to 832
The number of casualties from Indonesia's quake and tsunami came to 832, the agency in charge of managing the country's calamities said Sunday morning.
Numerous people trapped among the rubbles of the collapsed buildings were reported following the magnitude 7.5 quake that struck the country on Friday and spawned waves up to 6 meters high, Sutopo agency spokesman Purwo Nugroho said during a news conference.
The city of Palu, which has over 380,000 inhabitants, has been sprinkled with debris from the collapsed buildings.
Police arrest 32-year-old man on suspicion of murder after woman is stabbed to death
A murder investigation has been launched after a woman’s body was found this morning in Birkenhead, Merseyside.
The 44-year-old woman was found at 7.55 a.m
Police have urged people who may have seen or heard something in the area to get in touch.
Inspector Brian O'Hagan said: "The investigation is just beginning but I'd like to launch an appeal to anyone in the vicinity of John Street in Birkenhead and who's seen or heard something suspicious and invite them to contact us.
“I would also like to appeal to anyone, in particular taxi drivers, who may have recorded something on dashcam, to contact us because they may have very important information for our investigation.”
A police spokeswoman confirmed that the woman whose body was found is a resident of Birkenhead and was found inside a property.
This afternoon, some friends claiming to meet the woman arrived at the crime scene to ask questions about the site of the find this morning.
Inquiries are ongoing and police have said they are currently informing the victim's family members.
A taxi driver living in Grayson Mews has just tried to return to his apartment but was told by police that no one is allowed to enter or leave the building.
He was speechless when he found out what had happened.
Residents have been notified that it will be hours before they are allowed to return.
