Cromwell’s statue of Parliament becomes the last monument struck by the wave of ‘rewriting history’
His estrangement would be poetic justice for his Taliban destruction of so many religious and cultural objects of England performed by his fanatical Puritan followers.
But the Cromwell Society called Crick’s proposal “madness” and an “attempt to rewrite history.”
John Goldsmith, president of the Cromwell Society, said: "It was inevitable in the light of the current debate over the removal of statues that Oliver Cromwell's figure outside the Palace of Westminster became a target.
Iconoclasty during the British Civil Wars was not ordered or conducted by Cromwell.
Perhaps the wrong Cromwell would be sacrificed for the actions of his ancestor Thomas in the previous century.
Cromwell’s magnificent depiction of Sir William Hamo Thorneycroft is proof of 19th-century opinion and part of the historiography of a figure many believe should still be celebrated.
Goldsmith told The Sunday Telegraph: "Cromwell was considered by many, perhaps more at the end of the 19th century than today, a defender of parliament by external pressures, in his case of course the monarchy.
Whether it is an extremely accurate representation is the subject of an ongoing historical debate.
What is certain is that the mid-17th-century conflict defined the subsequent development of our nation, and Cromwell is an identifiable figure representing a part of that fracture.
His accomplishments as Lord protector must be celebrated and commemorated."
Murderous pig tears a Chinese farmer by killing him
A farmer has been attacked and killed by a pig in a market in south-western China according to local media reports.
The man, identified only with the surname "Yuan", was found dead with a severed artery, covered in blood near a pigsty at Liupanshui market in Guizhou province, the South China Morning Post reported on Sunday.
A pig farmer prepares to inject pork vaccines at a farm on May 30, 2005 in Xining in China's Qinghai province.
It is reported that he had travelled with his cousin from neighbouring Yunnan province on Wednesday to sell 15 pigs to the market.
The following morning, his cousin found him dead and discovered that the door of a nearby pigsty was open.
He claimed that in the pigsty there was a large male pig with blood on his face.
A forensic analysis confirmed that the 250-kg pig had mauled the breeder, according to statements.
“My cousin’s legs were covered in blood and mangled,” the cousin identified with the surname “Wu”, as reported in Guiyang Evening News.
Surveillance videos showed Yuan entering the market at 4.40 a.m
Her body was found about an hour later.
The animal that killed the man did not belong to Yuan or his cousin.
A market manager told the Evening News that the pig has been locked up to prevent from attacking other people, while police gather evidence at the crime scene.
Yuan's family and market authorities are trading a refund for the farmer's death.
Although they are rare, cases of attacks on humans by pigs have already been reported in the past.
In 2016, a pig attacked a woman and her husband on their farm in Massachusetts, causing serious injuries to the man.
Ten years earlier, a 300-kg pig nailed a Welsh farmer to his tractor until his wife managed to drive out the animal.
After an Oregon farmer was devoured by his pigs in 2012, a Manitoba breeder told CBC News that pigs are not usually aggressive but that the taste of blood can act as a “catalyst.”
“They just want to play.
They're playful, very curious .... they don't want to hurt.
It is enough to show them the right amount of respect,” he explained.
Hurricane Rosa's aftermaths will bring heavy heavy rains in the southwest U.S
As expected, Hurricane Rosa is losing strength as it moves on the cooler waters of Mexico’s northern coast.
However, Rosa will bring torrential rains over northern Mexico and the south-western coast of the USA in the coming days.
Rosa recorded winds of up to 138 km/h, a Category 1 hurricane, at 5 ET on Sunday and was 620 km south-west of Punta Eugenia, Mexico.
Rosa is expected to move north on Sunday.
In the meantime, a sacking is forming over the Pacific Ocean and moving east in the direction of the U.S. west coast. As Rosa approaches the Baja California Peninsula as a tropical storm, it will start to push a strong tropical humidity north, in the southwestern part of the U.S.
Rosa will carry up to 25 cm of rain in some areas of Mexico during the day on Monday.
Thus, the tropical humidity in contact with the approaching sacking will create heavy and vast precipitation in the south-west area in the coming days.
Locally, 2 to 10 cm of rain can cause dangerous sudden flooding, debris flows and possible landslides in the desert.
The tropical humidity will cause precipitation rates that will reach 2.5-7.5 cm per hour in some places, particularly in areas of southern Nevada and Arizona.
Precipitation of 2.5-10 cm of rain is expected in some areas of the south-west, particularly over much of Arizona.
Sudden flooding may occur with rapidly worsening conditions due to the widespread nature of tropical rainfall.
It is strongly discouraged to venture into the desert on foot in the presence of the threat of tropical rain.
Heavy rains could make river canyons in flood and thunderstorms will bring wind to puffs and raise dust.
Approaching sacking will cause heavy rain locally on part of the Southern California coast.
Precipitation of more than 1 cm will be possible, which could cause limited streams of debris and slippery carriageways.
This would be the region's first precipitation in the wet season.
Some scattered tropical rains will begin approaching Arizona towards the end of Sunday and the beginning of Monday, before the rains become more extensive at the end of Monday and early Tuesday.
Heavy rains will descend on the Four Corners on Tuesday to last until Wednesday.
In October there will be strong fluctuations in temperatures in the U.S. as the Arctic cools, but the tropics will remain warm enough.
Sometimes this leads to important changes in temperature over short distances.
An outstanding example of significant temperature differences in the U.S
There will be a difference of almost 20 degrees between Kansas City, Missouri and Omaha, Nebraska, and between St. Louis and Des Moines, Iowa.
Over the next few days, the residual heat of the summer will try to increase and expand again.
It is expected to start October for much of the central and eastern U.S., with 26 oC from the Southern Plains to areas of the north-east.
In New York it will be possible to reach 26 degrees on Tuesday, i.e. a temperature of about 12 degrees above average.
Our long-term climate predictions indicate a strong likelihood of above-average temperatures for the eastern U.S. until the first half of October.
Over 20 million people watched Brett Kavanaugh's hearing
Over 20 million people followed Thursday on six televised networks the gripping deposition by Supreme Court nominee Brett Kavanaugh and the woman who accused him of sexual assault that allegedly took place in the 1980s, Christine Blasey Ford.
In the meantime, the political impasse continues, with broadcasters interrupting regular programming for a last-minute breakthrough on Friday: an agreement devised by Arizona Senator Jeff Flake for the FBI to conduct a one-week investigation into the allegations.
Ford informed the Senate Judiciary Committee that it is 100% certain that Kavanaugh groped her intoxicated and tried to undress her during a party in high school.
Kavanaugh, during an inflamed deposition, said he was 100% certain that this did not happen.
More than the 20.4 million people indicated by Nielsen on Friday are likely to have followed the deposition.
The company was calculating the media audience on CBS, ABC, NBC, CNN, Fox News Channel and MSNBC.
The figures were not immediately available for the other networks that broadcast it, including PBS, C-SPAN and Fox Business Network.
And Nielsen usually has difficulty counting viewers who follow programs in offices.
To put everything into perspective, it’s an audience similar to that for a football playoff game or the Oscars.
Fox News Channel, whose pundits strongly supported Kavanaugh's nomination, led all the networks, with an average of 5.69 million viewers during the full-day hearing, Nielsen said.
ABC was the second-largest network with 3.26 million viewers.
CBS has recorded 3.1 million viewers, NBC 2.94 million, MSNBC 2.89 million and CNN 2.52 million, according to Nielsen.
Interest remained elevated after the hearing.
Flake was the central figure in Friday's drama.
After the moderate Republican's office issued a statement saying he would vote in favor of Kavanaugh, Flake was picked up by CNN and CBS operatives on Friday morning as he was being insulted by demonstrators as he tried to take an elevator toward a Justice Committee hearing.
He remained low-eyed for several minutes as he was reprimanded and broadcast live on CNN.
"I'm here right in front of you," a woman said.
“Do you think you’re telling the country the truth?
He has been told “You have the power while so many women are powerless.”
Flake claimed that his office had issued the statement and stated, before the elevator shut down, that it would have other things to say during the commission hearing.
All cable networks and broadcasters broadcast live a few hours later, when the Judiciary Committee voted to present Kavanaugh’s appointment to the entire senate for a vote.
But Flake asserted that she would do so only with the certainty that the FBI would analyze the allegations against the candidate over the course of the following week, as required by minority Democrats.
Flake was partially convinced by conversations with his friend, Democratic Senator Chris Coons.
Following a conversation with Coons and other senators later, Flake made his decision.
Flake's choice was important, because it was clear that the Republicans wouldn’t have the votes to approve Kavanaugh without the investigation.
President Trump has launched an FBI investigation into allegations against Kavanaugh.
