Volcano sprinkles with ashes Mexico City
The ashes leaking from the Popocatepetl volcano have reached the southern districts of Mexico's capital.
The National Centre for the Prevention of Calamities alerted Mexicans on Saturday to keep away from the volcano, after activity had increased in the crater and there were 183 emissions of gas and ashes in 24 hours.
The center was controlling the different rumbs and shocks.
Images on social media showed thin layers of ashes lining the windshields of automobiles in Mexico City's neighborhoods like Xochimilco.
Geophysicists have detected an increase in activity at the volcance located 45 miles (72 kilometers) southeast of the capital since a magnitude 7.1 quake struck the central area of Mexico in September 2017.
The volcano, known as "Don Goyo," has been active since 1994.
Clashes of the police with the Catalan separatists on the eve of the anniversary of the vote for independence
Six people were arrested in Barcelona on Saturday after pro-independence protesters clashed with police riot teams and thousands of people joined demonstrations by opponents to commemorate the first anniversary of the controversial Catalan vote on secession.
A group of masked separatists hampered by riot police targeted cops with eggs and dumped dust paint, creating dark clouds of dust in the streets usually overflowing with tourists.
Further scuffles occurred during the day with policemen who resorted to batons to contain the fight.
For several hours the groups of independents who sang “We will not forgive, we will not forgive” were confronted by Unionist demonstrators who cried “Long life to Spain.”
The local press reported that fourteen people had to receive treatment for minor injuries received during the demonstrations.
Tension remains high in the independence region a year after the referendum on 1 October considered illegal by the government in Madrid but recognized by the Catalan separatists.
Voters had voted with an overwhelming majority for independence, although turnout turned out to be low, and Catalans opposed to secession had extensively boycotted the vote.
According to the Catalan authorities, almost 1,000 people suffered injuries last year after police had tried to stop with violent clashes the referendum that was taking place at seats across the region.
Pro-independence groups had camped on Friday night to prevent a demonstration in support of the national police.
The demonstration took place but had to follow an alternative path.
Narcis Termes, 68, is an expecturist who participated in the separatist protest with his wife and said he had no more hope about the possibility of Catalonia becoming independent.
“Last year we experienced one of our best moments.
"I've seen my parents crying with joy for the chance to vote, but now everything is blocked," he explained.
Despite having achieved a pivotal victory although measuring in regional elections last December, Catalan independence parties have struggled to maintain momentum this year, with many of the movement’s best-known leaders finding a voluntary exile or in prison awaiting trial for their role in organizing the referendum and the subsequent declaration of independence.
Joan Puig, a 42-year-old mechanic who recorded with his phone the protest in support of the police, claimed that the conflict has been fueled by politicians from the two factions.
"The situation is increasingly tense," he said.
Oriol Junqueras, one of nine Catalan leaders in prison awaiting trial since the end of last year, has announced that he will run for election to the European Parliament elections next year.
“Presenting myself as a candidate in the European elections is the best way to denounce the involution of democratic values and repression by the Spanish government,” he said.
Londonderry: Men arrested after ramming a house with cars
Three men, aged 33, 34 and 39, were arrested after with a car they repeatedly slammed into a Londonderry home.
The incident occurred in Ballynagard Crescent on Thursday, at about 7:30pm BST.
Inspector Bob Blemmings said the damage was caused to the gates and the building itself.
It's also possible that the car was hit with a crossbow at one point.
Menga's attack marks Livingston's 1-0 win against Rangers
Dolly Menga's first goal with Livingston signed victory
Livingston already promoted left Rangers amazed by relegating Steven Gerrard to his second defeat in 18 games as Ibrox's club director.
Dolly Menga’s goal made the difference while Gary Holt’s team switched to the second division with Hibernian.
Gerrard's team remains therefore without an out-of-home victory in the Premiership this season and will have to face next Sunday the first in the Hearts standings, from which 8 points separate them.
On Thursday the Rangers hosted Rapid Vienna in the Europa League.
Meanwhile Livingston continue their series of wins in the division while remaining unbeaten for six games, with Coach Holt having yet to see a loss since replacing Kenny Miler last month.
Livingston wastes opportunities against unincisive guests
Holt's team should have been ahead well before the goal, with their immediacy causing the Rangers a lot of problems.
Scott Robinson smashed through but dragged his action in front of the goal and Alan Lithgow was only able to kick out after entering to meet Craig Halkett’s header on the other side of the goal.
The guests were content to let the Rangers play in front of them, knowing that they could put visitors in trouble with placed kicks.
And that's how the crucial goal was made.
The Rangers conceded a free kick and Livingston found an opening, Declan Gallagher and Robinson managed to move to Menga, who could score from the centre of the area.
Until that moment, the Rangers had dominated in possession of the ball but the defense of the host team had proved impenetrable and goalkeeper Liam Kelly hadn't had to worry too much,
This pattern remained during the second half, although Alfredo Morelos engaged Kelly in a parade.
Scott Pittman saw his goal-stroking attempt stopped by Rangers goalkeeper Allan McGregor and Lithgow carried out a shot out of another Livingston game scheme.
Crosses followed each other in the Livingston area and were continuously neutralized, while two penalty requests, after Halkett's contrast with substitute Glenn Middleton and one for foul-handing, were rejected.
A “phenomenal” performance from Livingston - analysis
Alasdair Lamont of BBC Scotland at Tony Macaroni Arena
A phenomenal performance and result for Livingston.
Until the last player, they were exceptional, continuing to exceed expectations along this exceptional trajectory.
Their style of play and collaborators have changed very little since their return to the maximum series, but you have to give a lot of credit to Holt for the way he has been able to spur the team since his arrival.
He's had so many heroes.
Captain Halkett was immense, at the head of a masterfully organised defense, while Menga put Connor Goldson and Joe Worrall in trouble throughout the match.
The Rangers, however, were uninspired.
Despite the excellent performance in some moments with Gerrard, in this case they have fallen well below those standards.
Missed the last pass - only once they managed to penetrate the opponent's area - and it can be considered a wake-up call for the Rangers, who are in the mid-rankings.
Erdogan received with mixed reactions in Cologne
There were smiles and blue skies on Saturday (September 29) during the meeting at breakfast of the leaders of Turkey and Germany in Berlin.
It was the last day of President Erdogan's controversial visit to Germany, aimed at dispensing relations between the NATO allies.
They clashed with issues such as human rights, freedom of the press and Turkey’s access to the EU.
Erdğğč headed to Cologne for the inauguration of a new huge mosque.
The city is home to Turkey's largest population group outside Turkey.
Police motivated with security reasons intervention to prevent a crowd of 25,000 from gathering in front of the mosque, but many supporters showed up nearby to see their president.
Hundreds of anti-Erdogan protesters, including numerous Kurds, made their voices heard, condemning Erdogan's policies and the German government's decision to welcome him to the country.
The protests mirror the dissent provoked by a visitor considered a hero by some Turks of Germany and called a despot by others.
Deptford car crash: Cyclist dies in clash with car
A cyclist died in a clash with a car in London.
The incident happened near the intersection of Bestwood Street and Evelyn Street, a busy Deptord street, in the city’s south-eastern area, at around 10:15 a.m.
The driver of the car stopped and an ambulance arrived on the spot, but the man died immediately.
The incident occurred months after another cyclist died, hit by a street pirate in Childers Street, about a mile from where Saturday's incident happened.
The Metropolitan Police said authorities are working to identify the man and inform his relatives.
Roads have been stranded and buses diverted and motorists have been advised to avoid the area.
Long Lartin Prison: Six guards injured during a clutter
Six prison guards were injured during a clutter at the maximum-security men's prison, the prison office said.
The turmoil erupted at Long Lartin's maximum-security men's prison in Worcestershire at 09:30 a.m
"Tornado" specialized officers were employed to manage the turmoil, which involves eight prisoners and is confined to a prison wing.
Officers were treated on the spot for minor injuries to the face.
A spokesman for the prison service said: "Specifily trained prison staff have been deployed to contain an incident currently underway at Long Lartin prison.
Six staff members were treated for injuries.
“We do not tolerate the presence of violence in our prisons and all those responsible will be reported to the police and could be sentenced to a longer sentence in prison.”
Long Lartin prison is home to over 500 inmates, including some of the country's most dangerous thugs.
In June it was announced that the director of the prison had to receive hospital treatment following the attack of a prisoner.
And in October last year, riot officers were called in to intervene in the prison to sedate a violent turmoil during which staff were attacked with pool balls.
Hurricane Rosa threatens Phoenix, Las Vegas, Salt Lake City with sudden flooding (which could benefit drought-affected areas)
It's rare for a tropical depression to hit Arizona, but it's exactly exactly what's probably going to happen early next week when Hurricane Rosa's last energies traverse the south-west of the desert, creating risks of sudden flooding.
The National Meteorological Service has already issued sudden flood alerts for Monday and Tuesday in western Arizona all the way to southern and eastern Nevada, southwestern California and Utah, including the cities of Phoenix, Flagstaff, Las Vegas and Salt Lake City.
Rosa is expected to head directly to Phoenix on Tuesday, approaching Monday in the evening with rains.
Phoenix’s National Meteorological Service said in a tweet that only “ten tropical cyclones have maintained the state of tropical storm or tropical depression within 200 miles from Phoenix since 1950!
Katrina (1967) was a hurricane within 40 miles from the Arizona border.
The most recent models of the National Hurricane Center predict precipitation of between 5 and 10 cm, with isolated peaks up to 15 cm in the Mogollon Rim in Arizona.
Other areas of the south-western area, including the Central Rocky Mountains and the Great Basin will receive 2.5 to 5 cm of precipitation, with isolated peaks of up to 10 cm.
For areas unrelated to the risk of sudden flooding, rain carried by Rosa could be a blessing as the region is hit by drought.
Although flooding is a very serious problem, some of this precipitation is likely to be positive since the south-western area is currently going through a period of drought.
According to the Drought Monitoring Center, just over 40 percent of Arizona is going through a period of extreme drought, the second-largest category.
Initially, Hurricane Rosa will be able to land through the Baja California peninsula of Mexico.
Rosa, who still maintained hurricane force on Sunday morning with winds at a top speed of 85 miles per hour, is 385 miles south of Punta Eugenia, Mexico and is moving north at 12 miles per hour.
The storm will collide with the coldest waters of the Pacific, thus losing potency.
So it's expected he'll arrive in Mexico with tropical storm force in the afternoon or Monday evening.
Precipitation on parts of Mexico could be intense, creating a high risk of flooding.
"Precipitation between 7.5 cm and 15 cm is expected from Baja California to the north-western area of Sonora, with a maximum of 25 cm," weather.com reported.
Rosa will then head north through Mexico as a tropical storm before reaching the Arizona border in the early morning hours of Tuesday with a state of tropical depression, then heading across Arizona and towards southern Utah late Tuesday evening.
“The main risk that may be caused by Rosa or its trawls are intense precipitation in Baja California, in the north-west area of Sonora, and in the south-western desert area of the United States,” the National Hurricane Center said in a statement.
Such rains are expected to generate life-threatening sudden flooding and debris flows into deserts, as well as landslides on mountainous terrain.
