The Senate's first pivotal procedural vote for Kavanaugh's nomination is expected to take place no later than Friday, a senior assistant at GOP management told CNN.
Hundreds of people killed by Indonesia earthquake and tsunami, and casualty figures continue to rise
At least 384 people were killed, many swept away by giant waves that hit the beaches, when an earthquake of great magnitude and a tassum struck the Indonesian island of Sulawesi, authorities claimed Saturday.
Hundreds of people had gathered for a Palu city beach festival on Friday, when waves up to 6 meters (18-foot) hit the beach at twilight, wiping out numerous people killing them and destroying anything in their path.
The tsunami followed a magnitude 7.5 quake.
“When the tsunami threat arose yesterday, people were still busy with their activities on the beach and didn’t run away immediately, thus turning into victims,” Purwo Nugroho, a spokeswoman for the Indonesian disaster control agency BNPB, told Jakarta.
"The tsunami didn't come alone, it dragged cars, logs, houses with it, it hit anything on the mainland," Nugroho said, adding that the tsunami moved offshore at a speed of 800 km/497 mile/hour before hitting the coast.
Some people climbed into trees to escape the tsunami and survived, he explained.
About 16,700 people were evacuated towards 24 centers in Palu.
Aerial photographs published by the Calamity Agency showed numerous buildings and shops destroyed, bridges twisted and collapsed and a mosque surrounded by water.
Aftershocks continued to shake the coastal city on Saturday.
Earthquake series has been felt in an area with 2.4 million.
The Indonesian Agency for the Evaluation and Application of Technology (BPPT) said in a communiqué that the energy released by Friday's strong earthquake amounted to nearly 200 times the power of the atomic bomb dropped on Hiroshima during World War II.
The geography of the city, which lies at the end of a long and narrow bay, may have boosted the size of the tsunami, he said.
Nugroho described the damages as “extended” and claimed that thousands of homes, hospitals, malls and hotels collapsed.
The bodies of some victims were found trapped under the rubble of collapsed buildings and added that 540 people were injured and 29 were missing.
Nugroho said the casualties and damage could be greater along the 300 km (190-mile) coast north of Palu, in an area called Donggala, closer to the epicenter of the earthquake.
Communications "have been totally paralyzed, with the absence of information" from Donggala, Nugroho said.
“There are over 300,000 people living in the area,” the Red Cross said, adding that its staff and volunteers were heading to the areas involved.
“This is already a tragedy, but it could become even more serious,” he said.
The agency was widely criticized on Saturday for failing to report that a tsunami had hit Palu, although authorities said the waves came at the time the alarm had been sounded.
In an amateur video shared on social media, you hear a man on an upper floor of a building crying out agitated way to alert people to the incoming tsunami in the street below.
A few minutes later a wall of water fell on the beach, wiping out buildings and cars.
Reuters was unabled to authenticate the video immediately.
The quake and tsunami resulted in a huge blackout that disrupted communications around Palu, making it difficult for the authorities to coordinate rescue interventions.
According to authorities, the army has started sending cargo planes with aid from Jakarta and other cities, but displaced people still need food and other basic necessities.
The city’s airport has only been reopened for rescue and will remain closed until October.
President Joko Widodo on Sunday scheduled a visit to evacuation centers in Palu.
Indonesia's Tsunami death toll rises to over 800.
That's terrible.
Although Donggala's World Vision staff managed to reach the city of Palu safely, where the employees took refuge under tarpaulin shelter in the courtyard of their office, during the commute they encountered scenes of devastation, Doseba said.
“They told me that they’ve seen so many houses destroyed,” he said.
That's terrible.
Even while the rescue groups have begun the difficult operations of starting the rescue gears, there have been some complaints about the fact that foreign rescue workers with extensive knowledge have been prevented from travelling to Palu.
The Indonesian regulations state that funding, supplies and staff from abroad can only begin to arrive if the site of a calamity has been declared an area of national catastrophe.
And this has not yet happened.
“This is still a provincial disaster,” said Aulia Arriani, spokesman for the Indonesian Red Cross.
"When the government says, "O.K., it's a national disaster," we can accept international assistance, but status has not yet been defined."
The second night in Palu after Friday's quake and tsunami, friends and families of the missing still maintained hopes that their loved ones would be part of the miracles that alleviate the sad stories of natural disasters.
On Saturday a child was pulled out of a drain.
On Sunday, rescuers freed a woman who had been stranded under the rubble for two days with her mother's body next door.
Gendon Subandono, the Indonesia's paragliding national team, had coached for the Asian Games that ended this month in Indonesia two of the missing paragliding drivers.
Others among the people trapped at the Roa Roa Hotel, including Mandagi, were his students.
“As a paraglider veteran, I’m carrying my emotional burden,” he said.
Gendon recounted how, in the hours following the spread of news related to the collapse of the Roa Roa Hotel within the paragliding community, he had desperately sent messages via WhatsApp to Palu's competitors, who were participating in the beach festival.
His messages, however, stopped at a single gray tick instead of two blue marks.
"I think it means the messages weren't delivered," he said.
Thieves steal $26,750 during an ATM replenishment at Newport on the Levee
On Friday morning thieves stole $26,750 from a Brink operator who was supplying an ATM to Newport on the Levee, according to a Newport Police Department press release.
The driver of the vehicle was emptying an ATM in the amusement center and was preparing to deliver more money, investigator Dennis McCarthy said in a statement.
While he was busy, another man “came out behind Brink’s employee” and stoled a bag with the money destined for delivery.
Witnesses have identified several suspects fleeing the crime scene, according to the communiqué, but police did not specify the number of people involved.
Anyone who has information about the identities of thugs is invited to contact the Newport Police at 859-292-3680.
Kanye West: Rapper changes his name to Ye
Rapper Kanye West is changing his name to Ye.
At announcing Saturday the name change on Twitter, he wrote: "The person formally known as Kanye West."
West, 41, has been dubbed Ye for a long time and used the nickname as the title of his eighth album, which came out in June.
The change came before his participation in Saturday Night Live, where he will present his new album, Yandhi.
It replaces in the program the singer Ariana Grande, who canceled her participation for "emotional reasons," declared the creator of the show.
In addition to being an abbreviation of his current professional name, West stated earlier that the word contains religious significance for him.
“I think ‘ye’ is the most widely used word in the Bible, and it means ‘you,’” West said earlier in the year, talking about the title of his album with radio presenter Big Boy.
"So it's you, it's us, it's about us.
We’ve gone from Kanye, which means the only one, to simply Ye—the reflection of our goodness, our malice, our confusion, everything.
“The album is more than the reflection of who we are.”
He's just one of several famous rappers who changed their name.
Sean Combs has been known as Puff Daddy, P. Diddy or Diddy, but this year has announced his preference for names Love and Brother Love.
An ex-collaborator from West, JAY-Z, has also changed the name to or without hyphen and capital letters.
President-elect of Mexico Andres Manuel Lopez Obrador has sworn not to use the army against the civilian population
Mexican President-elect Andres Manuel Lopez Obrador vowed never to use military force against the civilian population on the 50th anniversary of a bloody retaliation against students.
Lopez Obrador promised on Saturday in Tlatelolco Square to “never use the army to repress the Mexican people.”
Soldiers had fired on a peaceful demonstration in the square on October 2, 1968, killing 300 people at a time when left-wing student movements were taking hold across Latin America.
Lopez Obrador has pledged to support young Mexicans by handing out monthly grants to students and opening up more public universities.
He said that unemployment and lack of educational opportunities push young people towards criminal gangs.
U.S. is expected to double funding for AI
As China becomes increasingly active in the artificial intelligence industry, the U.S. is expected to double research funds in this field, says IA investor and operator Kai-Fu Lee, who worked for Google, Microsoft and Apple.
The comments follow the announcements of different U.S. government groups regarding AI, although in the U.S. there is generally a lack of a formal AI strategy.
Meanwhile, China unveiled its programme last year: it aspires to be #1 in AI innovation by 2030.
“Doubling the budget in AI research would be a good start considering that all the other countries are way behind the U.S., and we need a new revolution in AI,” Lee said.
Doubling funding could double the chances that the next big success in the AI industry will be achieved in the U.S., Lee told CNBC during an interview this week.
Lee, whose book "AI Superpowers: China, Silicon Valley and the New World Order" was published this month by Houghton publisher Mifflin Harcourt, is the CEO of Sinovation Ventures, which has invested in one of China's leading AI companies, Face++.
In the 1980s, at Carnegie Mellon University worked on an IA system that beat Othello's American player with the highest score and was an executive at Microsoft Research and president of Google's Chinese section.
Lee recalled previous U.S. government technology competitions such as the Defense Advanced Research Projects Agency's Robotics Challenge and asked when the next one will be held in order to identify new visionaries.
Researchers in the U.S. often have to work hard to be able to get funds from the government, Lee said.
“It’s not China that’s taking away academic leaders, it’s multinationals,” Lee said.
In recent years, Facebook, Google and other technology companies have hired luminaries from universities to work on AI projects.
Lee has stated that changes to immigration policy could help the U.S. boost its AI efforts.
"I think green cards should be offered automatically to PhD students in IA," he explained.
The Chinese State Council published its program for the development of the next generation of artificial intelligence in July 2017.
China's National Foundation of Natural Sciences provides funding to those working in academic institutions, similar to how the National Foundation for Sciences and Other Government Organizations distributes funds to U.S. researchers, bad quality of academic work is inferior in China, Lee explained.
Earlier this year, the U.S. Department of Defense created a Joint Center for Artificial AI, aimed at engaging industrial partners and universities and the White House announced the formation of a Special Commission for Artificial Intelligence.
And this month DARPA announced an investment of US$2 billion in an initiative called AI Next.
As for the NSF, it currently invests over 100 million dollars a year in AI research.
Meanwhile, the U.S. system aimed at creating a National Security Commission on Artificial Intelligence has not recorded activities for months.
Macedonians vote in referendum to change the country's name
The people of Macedonia voted on Sunday in a referendum on the possible change of the country's name to "Republic of North Macedonia," a move that would resolve the decades-long dispute with Greece that blocked its calls for membership of the European Union and NATO.
Greece, which owns a province called Macedonia, argues that the name of its neighbors constitutes a claim on its territory and has vetoed Macedonia’s entry to NATO and the EU.
The two governments struck a deal in June under the proposed new name, but nationalist opponents believe the change would damage the ethnic identity of the Slavic majority Macedonia population.
President Gjorge Ivanov has declared that he will not vote in the referendum and the boycott campaign has generated doubts about the possibility that the turnout reaches the minimum of 50% necessary for the validity of the referendum.
The question on the ballot of the referendum is as follows: "It agrees with membership of NATO and the EU and with the acceptance of the agreement with Greece."
Supporters of the name change, including Prime Minister Zoran Zaev, say it’s about the price to pay for Macedonia to be admitted to institutions such as the EU and NATO, one of the countries emerging from the collapse of the former Yugoslavia.
"I came today to vote for the future of the country, for the young people of Macedonia, so that they can live freely under the auspices of the European Union because this means a safer life for all of us," Olivera Georgijevska, 79, told Skopje.
Although it is not legally binding, a sufficient number of members of parliament have stated that it will respect the result of the vote to make it final.
Changing the name would require a two-third-party vesting majority.
The state electoral commission said there were no reports of irregularities until 1pm.
However, the turnout was only 16 per cent compared to 34% at the last parliamentary election in 2016, when 66% of registered voters had cast their vote.
“I’ve come to vote for my children, our place is in Europe,” said Gjose Tanevski, 62, a voter of the capital, Skopje.
The Prime Minister of Macedonia Zoran Zaev, his wife Zorica and their son Dushko voted for the referendum in Macedonia to change the name of the country that would open the door to accession to NATO and the European Union to Strumica, Macedonia, on September 30, 2018.
In front of the Paramento in Skopje, Vladimir Kavardarkov, 54, was preparing a small stage and settling the chairs in front of the tents mounted by those who want to boycott the referendum.
“We are for NATO and the EU, but we want to enter head-to-head, not for the door of service,” Kavadarkov said.
“We are a poor country, but we have dignity.
If they don’t want to accept us as Macedonia, we can turn to others like China or Russia and become part of Euro-Asian integration.”
Prime Minister Zaev says NATO membership will bring much-needed investment in Macedonia, which records an unemployment rate of more than 20%.
"I believe that the vast majority will be in favor because more than 80% of our citizens are in favor of the EU and NATO," Zaev said.
He stated that a “yes” would be the “confirmation of our future.”
A poll published last Monday by the Macedonian Institute for Political Analysis reported that between 30 and 43% of voters would participate in the referendum - a percentage below the required turnout.
Another poll conducted by Telma TV of Macedonia, indicated that 57% of those surveyed planned to go to vote on Sunday.
Of this 57%, 70% said they would vote for yes.
In order for the referendum to be valid, the turnout must be 50% plus a vote.
A failure of the referendum would represent the first serious blow to pro-Western government policy since taking office in May last year.
Look: Manchester City's Sergio Aguero scores a goal by avoiding Brighton's entire defence
Sergio Aguero and Raheem Sterling took care of Brighton's defence during the 2-0-win match by Manchester City on Saturday at Etihad Stadium in Manchester, England.
Aguero made it seem extremely easy his point scored at the 65th minute.
