Scherzer, who would be the first pitcher for the 34th time, on Thursday performed a launching session and would be the first pitcher on his normal retirement Sunday.
The Washington pitcher is 18-7 with an ERA of 2.53 ERA and 300 strikeouts in 220 2/3 innings this season.
Trump at a rally in West Virginia
The President indirectly referred to the situation surrounding its Supreme Court nominee Brett Kavanaugh as he talked about the importance of turnout for Republicans at the midterm elections.
"Everything we've done is put into play in November.
We are five weeks away from one of the most important elections of our lives.
It’s one of the big, big ones — I’m not a candidate, but I’m really working for it, I’m fighting for excellent candidates,” he said.
Trump went on to say, "Let's see this horrible group of radical Democrats, it's happening right now.
And they are determined to take back power using any necessary means, you can see malice, pettiness.
They don’t care who they’re able to hit, who they have to overwhelm in order to regain power and control, because that’s what they want, power and control, and we won’t give them.”
The Democrats, he said, gave themselves the mission to “resist and obstruct.”
"And it's been possible to see him in the last four days," he said, calling Democrats "angry, mean, petty and liars."
He referred to the Senate Judiciary Committee with Democratic Senator Dianne Feinstein by name, unleashing loud whistles among the public.
“Do you remember his answer?
Did you leak the document?
Um, uh, what.
No, no, I'm looking forward to a - that was bad body language - the worst body language I've ever seen."
The Labour Party is no longer very tolerant.
He doesn't tolerate people saying what they're thinking
When the Momentum activists of my local party voted to censor me, it wasn't a big surprise.
After all, they are the latest in a number of members of the Labour parliament who have been notified that they are not welcome, and all because they have said what they were thinking.
My parliamentary colleague Joan Ryan received similar treatment because he resolutely opposed anti-Semitism.
In my case, the censorship motion criticized me to express my disagreement with Jeremy Corbyn.
On the importance of a responsible economic policy, on national security, on Europe, ironically all subjects similar to those on which Jeremy disagreed with previous leaders.
The notice for the Nottingham East Labour meeting on Friday states that "we want meetings to be inclusive and productive."
For most of my eight years as a member of parliament of the local Labour Party, Friday night meetings were exactly like this.
However today this is not the tone of numerous meetings and the promise of a “kinder and more courteous” policy has been forgotten for a long time, if it has ever been remembered.
It has become increasingly obvious that different viewpoints are not tolerated in the Labour Party and each opinion is judged on whether or not it is acceptable to party leadership.
All of this began shortly after Jeremy became the head of the party, and colleagues with whom I previously thought of sharing a similar political vision began to expect a turnaround on my part and to take positions that I would never accept, whether it was national security or the European single market.
When I speak in public - and it doesn't really matter what I say - they follow invectives of insults on social media calling for my withdrawal, denouncing the politics of the center and telling me I shouldn't be part of the Labour party.
It’s not just my experience.
I certainly know that I am luckier than my other colleagues since the comments directed to me tend to be of a political nature.
I am admired by the professionalism and determination of those colleagues who have to face a creek of insults with a sexist or racist background every day but who do not allow themselves to be intimidated.
One of the most disappointing aspects of this political era is the normalization of levels of insults.
Jeremy Corbyn said last week that the Labour Party should promote a culture of tolerance.
The reality is that we are no longer in a tolerantive environment and with every motion of no confidence or modification of the rules of selection, the party shrinks.
In the last two years I have been given a lot of advice, urging me to lower my header, not to express my opinions so openly and everything “would be fine”.
But it's not to do what I've gotten into politics.
Since I joined the Labour Party 32 years ago as an alumni, provoked by the neglect of the Thatcher government that had left my secondary school class literally fall apart, I have tried to defend better public services for those who need it most, as a local councillor or government minister.
I've never hid my political views, not even at the last election.
No one in Nottingham East may have been baffled in any way by my political positions and points of disagreement with the current leadership.
To those who promoted the motion on Friday, I would like to say that when the country is plunging towards a Brexit that will harm households, companies and our public services, I do not understand the desire to waste time and energy talking about my loyalty to the head of the Labour party.
But in reality the only message I have is not for Nottingham Momentum, but for my constituents, whether they are members of the Labour Party or not: I am proud to serve you and I promise that no threat of exclusion or political opportunity will stop me from acting in defense of what I believe to be your best interests.
Chris Leslie is a member of parliament for Nottingham East
