'"The sea is ours": Bolivia landlocked hopes that a court will reopen the path to the Pacific
Sailors are patrolling a taründen naval headquarters in La Paz.
Public buildings display an ocean-blue flag.
The naval bases from Lake Titicaca up to the river of the Amazoni are decorated with the motto: "The sea is ours by right.
Retrieving it is a duty."
Acrogained across the sea, the memory of the lost coast and now in the hands of Chile after a bloody conflict over 19th-century resources is still alive, as is the desire to once again sail the Pacific Ocean.
These hopes have perhaps reached the highest level in decades, while Bolivia is awaiting the decision of the International Court of Justice on 1 October after five years of deliberations.
"Bolivia has the momentum, a spirit of unity and serenity and obviously is waiting for the result with a positive attitude," Roberto Calzadilla, a Bolivian diplomat, explained.
Many Bolivians will look at the judgment of the International Court of Justice on large screens across the country, in the hope that the Hague tribunal favorably accommodates Bolivia’s claim that – after decades of intermittent dialogue – Chile is obliged to negotiate, guaranteeing Bolivia an outlet to the state’s competence.
Evo Morales, Bolivia's charismatic indigenous president - which is facing a controversial re-election battle next year - has much at stake with Monday's ruling.
“We are very close to our return to the Pacific Ocean,” he promised at the end of August.
But some analysts believe that the tribunal is unlikely to decide in favor of Bolivia and that little would change if it did.
The U.N. body based in the Netherlands has no power to allocate Chilean territory and has specified that it will not determine the outcome of possible negotiations.
The fact that the judgment of the International Court of Justice comes just six months after the final arguments indicates that the case ‘wasn’t complicated,” said Paz Zárate, a Chilean expert in international law.
And far from advancing the Bolivian cause, the past four years may have delayed it.
“The problem of access to the sea has been monopolized by the current Bolivian administration,” Zárate said.
Morale's belligerent retorication slackened any remaining good intention of Chile, he suggested.
Bolivia and Chile will continue negotiations at some point, but it will be very difficult to maintain discussions as a result of what happened.
The two countries haven't exchanged ambassadors since 1962.
Former President Eduardo Rodríguez Veltzé, Bolivia’s representative in The Hague, rejected the idea that the tribunal’s decision-making process was particularly swift.
On Monday Bolivia will be able to “enjoy extraordinary opportunities in relations with Chile” and will have the opportunity to “put an end to 139 years of disagreements with mutual benefit”, he explained.
Calzadilla also denied that Morales - one of Latin America's most popular presidents - is using the maritime issue as a political escamotage.
“Bolivia will never give up its right to have access to the Pacific Ocean,” he added.
“Sentencing is an opportunity to see that we need to overcome the past.”
North Korea says nuclear disarmament won’t happen without confidence in the U.S
North Korean Foreign Minister Ri Yong Ho has said that his nation will never carry out disarmament from nuclear weapons if he cannot trust Washington first.
Ri spoke Saturday at the U.N. General Assembly.
