Washington distrusts issuing the statement without Pyongyang carrying out major disarmament actions first.
Both Kim and U.S. President Donald Trump want a second summit.
But there is widespread skepticism that Pyongyang intends to give up an arsenal that the country probably considers the only way to ensure its own security.
Pompeo is planning a visit to Pyongyang next month to prepare the second summit between Kim and Trump.
Parisian fashion shows feature the newest trend in huge headgears that will soon be in a haute-fashioned store close to you
If you want to enlarge your collection of headdresses or completely block the rays of the sun, look no further.
Designers Valentino and Thom Browne unveiled a series of crazy oversized headgears for their spring-summer 2019 collection that impressed the world of fashion at Paris Fashion Week.
Extremely impractical hats have burgled on Instagram this summer and these stylists have sent their captivating creations on the catwalk.
Valentino's main piece was an exaggerated beige-colored hat decorated with a wide feather-like edge that submerged the models' heads.
Other oversized accessories included jeweled watermelons, a sorcerer's hat and even a pineapple - but they weren't designed to keep your head warm.
Thom Browne also presented a selection of bizarre masks, just in time for Halloween.
Many of these colored masks feature sewn lips and look more like a Hannibal Lecter representation than haute couture.
One creation resembled an immersion suit with a mask and goggles, while another looked like a melted ice cream cone.
And if you go on with the huge statements of style, you're lucky.
Style observers predict that huge caps could get up to yourself in the streets of high fashion.
Outsize hats arrive in the wake of “La Bomba”, the straw hat with a tense more than a meter wide that has been seen on anyone, from Rihanna to Emily Ratajkowski.
The cult label behind the very impractical hat that invaded social media sent another huge creation on the catwalk: a straw beach bag almost as big as the costume model who wears it.
The burnt orange raffia bag, finished with raffia fringe and with a white leather handle was the highlight of Jacquemus' La Riviera's spring-summer 2019 collection at Paris Fashion Week.
celebrity fashion designer Luke Armitage told FEMAIL: “I anticipate that these big hats and beach bags will arrive in luxury stores next summer, as the designer got such a big impact that it would be difficult to ignore the demand for oversized accessories.”
John Edward: Language skills are essential for global citizens
The independent Scottish schools maintain a curriculum of academic excellence and this continued in 2018 with another set of outstanding results at the exams, reinforced by individual and collective success in sport, art, music and other collective efforts.
With more than 30,000 pupils across Scotland, these schools, represented by The Scottish Council of Independent Schools (SCIS), aspire to provide the best level of service to their students and their parents.
Independent schools aspire to prepare pupils for subsequent higher education, their chosen careers and their place in the world as global citizens.
As an area of education capable of developing and adopting a personalized school study plan, we observe that modern languages continue to be a popular subject and required within schools.
Nelson Mandela said: "If you speak to a man in a language he understands, the message will get to his brain.
If you speak to him in his mother tongue, the message will get to the heart.”
This is a powerful reminder that we can’t rely only on English if we want to create trusting relationships with people from other countries.
From this year’s exam results, we can see that languages are at the top of rankings, with the highest success rates within independent schools.
68% of the pupils who studied foreign languages got the highest marks.
The data, collected by the 74 SCIS member schools, showed that 72% of the students obtained the highest grade with honors in Chinese, while 72% of those who studied German, 69% of those who studied French and 63% of those who studied Spanish obtained the maximum grade.
This demonstrates that independent schools in Scotland are promoting foreign languages as key skills for children and young people, who will undoubtedly need them in the future.
Currently languages, as a choice of matter, are considered at the same level as STEM subjects (science, technology, technique and mathematics) in the study plans of independent institutes and elsewhere.
A survey conducted by the UK Commission on Employment and Skills in 2014 found that the reasons that employees indicated for the difficulty in covering jobs are attributable in 17% of cases to a lack of language skills.
For this reason, language skills are becoming increasingly essential to prepare young people for their future careers.
With more and more job opportunities that require language proficiency, these skills are essential in a globalized world.
Regardless of your chosen career, if you have learned a second language, you will have an effective advantage in the future with a lifetime proficiency like this.
The ability to communicate directly with people from other countries will bring a multilingual person automatically to the top of the competition.
According to a YouGov survey of more than 4,000 adults in the UK in 2013, 75% were unable to speak a foreign language at a level sufficient to maintain a conversation and French was the only two-digit language.
For this reason, investing in language teaching is so important for today’s children.
Knowledgment of multiple languages, especially those of developing economies, will equip children with a better chance of finding valid employment.
In Scotland, each school will differ in the languages taught.
Several schools will focus on the most classic modern languages, while others will teach languages that are considered the most important for the United Kingdom looking to 2020, such as Mandarin Chinese or Japanese.
Whatever the interest of your child, there will always be several languages to choose from in independent schools, with the teaching staff specialized in this area.
Independent Scottish schools are dedicated to providing a learning environment that will prepare children and equip them with the skills necessary for success, whatever their future.
It is not possible to deny at this time, in a global trading system, that languages continue to be fundamental for the future of the country, and this must be reflected in teaching.
In fact, modern languages should be considered “international communicative competences.”
Independent schools will continue to offer this choice, diversity and excellence for young people in Scotland.
Faut bien le faire.
John Edward is the Director of the Scottish Council of Independent Schools
