The moment of the gathering a West Virginia of Trump, YouTube channel
President Donald Trump starts tonight in Wheeling, West Virginia at a flurry of rallies.
This is the first meeting of the five planned for Trump in the next week, including stops in friendly resorts such as Tennessee and Mississippi.
With the pending confirmation vote for his choice to cover the vacancy on the Supreme Court, Trump will try to create support for the upcoming midterm elections as Republicans run the risk of losing control of Congress in November's election.
At what time will it be Trump's rally and how is it possible to follow it online?
Trump's rally in Wheeling, West Virginia, is scheduled for 7pm tonight, Saturday, September 29, 2018.
You can follow Trump's rally in West Virginia online to via live broadcast on YouTube.
Trump will likely talk about this week's hearings of Supreme Court nominee Brett Kavanaugh, who have become strained due to sexual assault charges with an up-and-suppling Senate confirmation vote for a week as the FBI conducts the investigation.
But the main purpose of this set of rallies is to help the Republicans who face the November election to get some push.
President-Trump's campaign said that these five rallies over next week are aimed at "enhancing volunteers and supporters as the Republicans seek to protect and expand the majorities they hold in the Senate and the House of Deputies," according to Reuters.
“Congressional control is so decisive for its program that the president will travel to as many states as we move into the busiest season of the campaign,” Trump’s campaign spokesman told Reuters.
Scheduled for Wheeling’s Wesbanco Arena, tonight’s rally could recall supporters from “Ohio and Pennsylvania and attract Pittsburgh media coverage,” according to West Virginia Metro News.
On Saturday it will be the second time in the last month Trump will visit West Virginia, the state where he had won by more than 40 percentage points in 2016.
Trump is trying to help West Virginia's Republican Senate nominee, Patrick Morrisey, who is queuing in polls.
“It’s not a good sign for Morrisey that the president has to come to try to give him a boost in the polls,” Said Simon Haeder, a political scientist at West Virginia University.
Ryder Cup 2018: U.S
After three one-sided sessions, Saturday afternoon quartets may have been what this Ryder Cup needed.
Oscillating pendulum motion is a totally made-up sporting concept in which players firmly and never believe as in races like these.
Where would the motion be now, then?
“They had a six-point lead and now it’s four, so I’d say we’re conserving a bit of motion,” Jordan Spieth said as she walked away at the end of the day.
Europe is ahead, of course, four points ahead with another twelve at stake.
The Americans feel they have a bit of wind in favor, as Spieth said, and they have a lot to be hopeful for, not least the form of Spieth and Justin Thomas who played together all day and each boasts three points out of four.
Spieth was ruthless from tee to green and is setting an example.
Guttural screams of celebration got stronger as the match went on, sinking a crucial putt to tie the scores at game four, when he and Thomas were down by two points after two.
His putt who made them win the game at the 15 hole was greeted with a similar scream, the one who tells you he believes the American team isn't out of the race yet.
"You really have to try your best to worry about your game," Spieth said.
That's all that each of these players can do now.
18 holes to make a point.
The only players with more points than Spieth and Thomas in the past two days are Francesco Molinari and Tommy Fleetwood, the irrefutable version of the Ryder Cup.
The strange but adorable couple of Europe is four from four and can’t do anything wrong.
"Moliwood" were the only couple who didn't pull a bogey on Saturday afternoon, but they also avoided bogeys on Saturday morning, Friday afternoon and the ninth hole on Friday morning.
That course, and the way their energy seems to flow to and from the boisterous crowd solidifies the certainty that they are the players to beat on Sunday, and there wouldn’t be a more popular player than Fleetwood or Molinari to seal an eventual European winning as the sun sets on Le Golf National.
Preferably both simultaneously on different holes.
However, European glory speeches are still premature.
Bubba Watson and Webb Simpson soon joined Sergio Garcia, the hero of the morning four-ball, when he was paired with Alex Noren.
A bogey and two double bogets on the first nine holes sunk the Spaniard and the Swede in a hole from which they couldn't even remotely climb back.
However, there is no one on Sunday to help you get out of your hole.
Fourballs and foursomes are so fascinating to closely observe due to the interactions between the couples, the advice they give and that they don’t give and for the way the strategy can change at a time.
Europe has played better as a team so far and gained an important advantage in the final day but this foursome session has shown that the USA team has the courage to fight, which some, in particular Stateside, have doubted.
Europe with a 10-6 lead in the Ryder Cup final day
Europe will have a solid lead in the Ryder Cup's final day after concluding Saturday's fourball and foursome matches with a 10-6 lead over the United States.
The inspired pair composed of Tommy Fleetwood and Francesco Molinari led the charge with two wins over a struggling Tiger Woods to bring the score so far at Le Golf National to four-points.
Thomas Bjorn's European team, in an attempt to retain the trophy lost in Hazeltine two years ago, dominated on an uneven American team in morning fourballs, winning 3-1.
The U.S
Jim Furyk's team need eight points from Sunday's 12 singles matches to retain the trophy.
Fleetwood is the first European debutant to win four points in a row while he and Molinari, known as "Molliwood" after a sensational weekend are the second pair to win four points from the four opening games in Ryder Cup history.
After crushing Woods and Patrick Reed in the fourballs, they worked superbly to beat akout a downed Woods and American debutant Bryson Dechambeau with an even clearer 5&4.
Woods, who dragged himself on for two games on Saturday, showed occasional flashes of brilliance but lost 19 of his 29 games in fourballs and foursomes and seven in a row.
Justin Rose, at rest during the morning's fourballs, returned alongside partner Henrik Stenson in the foursomes to beat 2&1 Dustin Johnson and Brooks Koepka - number one and three in the world rankings.
However, Europe did not achieve all the desired results in a pleasant and ventilated day south-west of Paris.
Three-times big winner Jordan Spieth and Justin Thomas set the benchmark for Americans with two points on Saturday.
They secured a gritty 2&1 win over Jon Rahm and Spain's Ian Poulter in the fourballs and returned later to beat Poulter and Rory McIlroy 4&3 in the foursomes, having lost the opening two holes.
Only twice in Ryder Cup history has a team returned from a four-point gap moving to the singles, although as a starter, Furyk's team only needed a draw to defend the trophy.
After being the best seconds for two days, however, a counterattack on Sunday appears to be out of their reach.
North Korea absolutely refuses unilateral disarmament without trust
North Korea’s foreign minister told the U.N. on Saturday that the maintenance of sanctions was escalating the country’s distrust in the U.S
Ri Yong Ho told at the General Assembly of the World Body that North Korea adopted “important goodwill measures” during the past year, such as halting nuclear and missile tests, dismantling the site for nuclear tests, and a commitment to non-proliferation of nuclear weapons and nuclear technology.
“However, we don’t see any compliant responses from the United States,” he said.
"Without trust in the U.S
Although Ri has re-used the well-known complaints regarding Washington’s resistance to a “in-stage” denuclearization with which North Korea would be rewarded based on gradual actions, its declaration was important because it did not reject unilateral denuclearization as Pyongyang did in the past.
Ri referred to a joint statement issued by Kim Jong Un and Dondal Trump during the first upbring meeting between an incumbent U.S. president and a North Korean leader, held in Singapore on June 12, when Kim pledged to work towards “denuclearization of the Korean Peninsula,” while Trump pledged guarantees to North Korean security.
North Korea has sought a formal ending agreement at the 1950-53 Korean War, but the U.S. has claimed Pyongyang must waive its nuclear weapons first.
Washington has also objected to appeals to ease the strict international sanctions imposed on North Korea.
“The U.S. insists on the need to ‘have denuclearization first’ and to increase the level of pressure with sanctions to achieve its purpose coercively and even oppose the “end-of-war declaration,” Ri said.
“The idea that sanctions can bring us to our knees is an illusion of those who don’t know anything about us.
But the problem is that the continuation of sanctions is increasing our mistrust.”
Ri did not refer to programs of a second meeting between Kim and Trump that the U.S. leader had announced at the U.N. earlier this week.
The minister instead highlighted the three meetings between Kim and South Korean leader Moon Jae-in over the past 5 months and added: "If the counterpart to this issue of denuclearization was South Korea and not the United States, the denuclearization of the Korean peninsula would not be at this dead end."
But even so, the tone of Ri’s speech was appreciably different than last year at the U.N. General’s General Assembly, when he stated that it was inevitable to target U.S. territory with North Korea’s missiles after “President of Evil” Trump had called Kim a “missile man” engaged in a suicide mission.
Before the United Nations Trump, who last year had threatened to "fully destroy" North Korea, poured praise on Kim for his courage in embarking on the path of disarmament, but declared that much more needs to be done and that the sanctions will remain in force until North Korea's denuclearization.
On Wednesday Trump said he had no deadline, stating that “if it takes two years, three years or five months, it doesn’t matter.”
Cine and Russia say the U.N. Security Council should reward Pyongyang for its actions.
However, U.S. Secretary of State Mike Pompeo told the U.N
The Security Council unanimously backed sanctions control North Korea in 2006 in an effort to suffocate funding of nuclear and ballistic missile programs.
Pompeo met with Ri on the sidelines of the U.N
Pompeo has visited North Korea three times this year, but the last trip didn’t go well.
He left Pyongyang in July stating that progress had been made, but a few hours later North Korea had accused him of presenting “gangster requests.”
North Korea had pledged during a meeting this month with Moon to dismantle a missile site and a nuclear complex if the U.S. adopted “compliant measures.”
He stated that Kim had explained to him that the “compliant measures” he wished for were guarantees of security to which Trump had engaged in Singapore and actions in the direction of a normalization of relations with Washington.
Harvard students take a course on how to rest enough
A new course at Harvard University this year prompted all of its students to sleep more in an effort to counteract the growing virile culture of studying for whole nights fueled by caffeine.
A university scholar found that students at the world’s number one university often have no idea of the basic concepts of caring for themselves.
Charles Czeisler, professor of sleep medicine at Harvard Medical School and a specialist at Brigham and Women's Hospital, has developed the course, which he believes to be the first of its kind in the U.S.
He came up with the idea of starting the course after a conference he had given on the impact of lack of sleep on learning.
“In the end a girl came to me and said, ‘Why am I being told this only now, when I’m a senior?’
She said that no one had ever told her anything about the importance of sleep, which surprised me very much,” she told The Telegraph.
The course, given for the first time this year, explains to students the essential concepts of how good sleep-related habits can help academic and athletic performance, as well as improve overall well-being.
Paul Barreira, professor of psychiatry at Harvard Medical School and the university’s director-general of health services, said that the university decided to introduce the course after discovering that students were suffering from a severe lack of sleep during the week.
The one-hour course includes a number of interactive activities.
In a section there is the image of a dormitory room, where students can select cups of coffee, tents, sneakers and books to learn about the effects of caffeine and light and how athletic performance is influenced by lack of sleep, and the importance of an evening routine.
In another section, participants are explained how long-term lack of sleep can increase the risks of heart attacks, stroke, depression and cancer.
A map of the campus, with interactive icons, therefore encourages participants to think about their daily routine.
“We know it won’t immediately change student behavior.
But we believe that they have the right to know, just as you have the right to know the health effects of the choice of smoking cigarettes,” added Prof. Czeisler.
The culture of pride linked to “white night to study”, still exists, he said, adding that modern technology and the increasing pressure on students lead lack of sleep to be an ever-increasing problem.
Getting enough sleep and good-quality sleep should be the “secret weapon” of the students to combat stress, exhaustion, anxiety, he explained, and even should serve to avoid gaining weight because the lack of sleep brings the brain into starvation mode, making people constantly hungry.
Raymond So, a 19-year-old California student enrolled in the Faculty of Chemistic Biology and Physics, helped Professor Czeisler develop the course, after taking one of his lectures last year during his first year at Harvard.
He explained that the course had opened his eyes and had inspired him to promote a course aimed at the entire campus.
The next step, he hopes, will be to ask all graduate students to follow a similar study program before enrolling at this competitive institution.
Professor Czeisler advised students to set an alarm clock for when they have to go to bed, as well as for when they have to wake up, and to be aware of the harmful effects of the “blue light” emitted by electronic screens and LED lights, which can tilt the circadian rhythm, leading to sleep problems.
Livingston 1 - 0 Rangers: Menga's goal knocks down Gerrard's men
The Rangers suffered another bounty of depression from away with Dolly Menga's goal relegating Steven Gerrard's disjointed team to a 1-0 defeat at Livingston.
Ibrox's team were looking for their first win away from February's 4-1 triumph at St Johnstone, but Gary Holt's team inflicted Gerrard's second defeat in 18 games as a coach leaving his team eight-points clear of Ladbrokes Premiership leaders, the Hearts.
Menga scored seven minutes before half-time and a short-inspired Rangers line-up has never seemed able to draw.
As the Ranger now falls to sixth place, Livingston rises to third, and only behind Hibernian by goal difference.
And there could be further problems coming for Rangers after linesman Calum Spence was treated for a head injury due to an object apparently thrown from the part away from home.
Gerrard made eight changes to the team, which quickly surpassed Ayr in the semi-finals of the Betfred Cup.
Holt, on the other hand, chose the usual line-up that snatched a point from Hearts last week and would be pleased with the way his well-prepared team choked their opponents at every lap.
Rangers may have dominated by possession of the ball but Livingston took better advantage of the balls at his disposal.
They could have scored just after two minutes when Menga’s filter pass in the first half sent Scott Pittman forward towards Allan McGregor’s goal but the midfielder wasted the chance.
Keaghan Jacobs' deep-fighting ballot then found captain Craig Halkett but his defending partner Alan Lithgow could only pull to the side on the second post.
The Rangers took control but it seemed there was more hope than conviction in their game in the penalty area.
Alfredo Morelos certainly felt he had to see himself assigned a penalty at the expiration of the 15th following a clash with Steven Lawless but referee Steven Thomson ignored the Colombian's demands.
Rangers only managed to pull twice into goal in the first half but former Ibrox goalkeeper Liam Kelly didn’t let worry about Lassana Coulibaly’s header and Ovie Ejaria’s bland shot.
Although Livingston's 34th-minute opening goal may have been unexpected, no one can deny he deserved it even just for his commitment.
Rangers again failed to handle a football placed deep by Jacobs.
Scott Arfield didn't react when Declan Gallagher filtered the ball to Scott Robinson, who kept calm in choosing Menga for a clean conclusion.
Gerrard acted during the interval replacing Coulibaly with Ryan Kent and the gearbox had an almost immediate impact as the winger tucked a ball into Morelos, but the exceptional Kelly threw himself off the goal line to block it.
But the Livingston went on to drag visitors exactly into the kind of game they love, with Lithgow and Halkett picking up one long ball after another.
Holt's team may have increased the gap in the final stages, but McGregor objected to stopping Jacobs before Lithgow pulled out of the corner.
Rangers replacement Glenn Middleton filed a last-date penalty request after a junction with Jacobs but Thomson denied it again.
Almanac: the inventor of the Geiger meter
And now a page from our "Sunday Morning": September 30, 1882, 136 years ago today and the COUNT continues... the day when future physicist Johannes Wilhelm "Hans" Geiger was born in Germany.
Geiger developed a method for detecting and measuring radioactivity, an invention that made the device known as Geiger counter.
A staple of science ever since, the Geiger counter has also become a pillar of pop culture, when in the 1950 film "Bells of Coronado," starring those unbelievable cowboy scientists Roy Rogers and Dale Evans:
Man: "What the hell is that?"
Rogers: “It’s a Geiger counter, used to detect radioactive minerals, such as uranium.
When you put on these earbuds you can really feel the effects of the atoms released by radioactivity into minerals.”
Evans: “Say it’s just bursting now!”
"Hans" Geiger died in 1945, just a few days after his 63rd birthday.
But the invention that bears his name continues to live.
New cancer vaccine can teach immune system to 'see' malignant cells
A new cancer vaccine may teach the immune system to “see” malignant cells and kill them
Vaccine teaches the immune system to recognize malignant cells as part of treatment
The method involves the extraction of immune cells from a patient, and their alteration in the laboratory
They can then “see” a protein common to numerous types of tumor and are then reinjected
An experimental trial of this vaccine is yielding promising results in patients with different types of cancer.
A woman treated with the vaccine who teaches the immune system to recognize malignant cells has seen her ovarian cancer disappear for over 18 months.
The method involves extracting immune cells from a patient and altering them in the laboratory so they can “see” a protein common to numerous types of cancer, called HER2, and then those cells are injected again.
Professor Jay Berzofsky, of the U.S. National Cancer Institute based in Bethesda, Maryland, said: "Our findings indicate that we have a very promising vaccine."
The HER2 protein “promotes the growth of different types of cancer”, including breast, ovaries, lungs and colorectals, explained Prof Berzofsky.
A similar immune cell extraction procedure from patients to “teach” them how to target cancer cells worked in the treatment of a type of leukemia.
Kanye West launches into a pro-Trump diatribe wearing a MAGA (Make America Great Again) cap after his appearance on SNL.
It didn't go well
Kanye West was booed in the studio during an episode of Saturday Night Live after a disconclusive performance during which he praised U.S. President Donald Trump and claimed he would run in 2020.
After playing his third song of the evening, called Ghost Town, during which he wore a MAGA cap, he began a rage against the Democrats and reiterated his support for Trump.
"So many times I talk to white people and they say to me, 'As you like Trump, he's a racist!'
Well, if I was worried about racism, I'd have left America a long time ago," he explained.
The SNL began the show with a skit whose protagonist was Matt Damon, in which the Hollywood star mocked Brett Kavanaugh's testimony before the Senate Judiciary Committee against the allegations of sexual assault filed by Christine Blasey Ford.
