Allegation of attempted murder for stabbing at Belfast restaurant
A 45-year-old man has been charged with attempted murder after a man was stabbed at an east Belfast restaurant on Friday.
The incident occurred in Ballyhackamore, according to police.
The accused is due to show up at the breaching in Belfast on Monday.
The charges will be reviewed by the Public Prosecutor’s Office.
Sword Throne Star Kit Harington Attacks Toxic Masculinity
Kit Harington is known for his role as a fighter Jon Snow in the violent medieval fantasy series Game of Thrones.
But the 31-year-old actor attacked the stereotype of the hero macho, stating that such on-screen soils often make boys believe they have to be tough to be respected.
In an interview with The Sunday Times Culture, Kit said he believed that “something went wrong” and reflected on how to deal with the problem of toxic masculinity in the #MeToo era.
Kit, who recently got married to Sword Throne co-star, 31, admitted to worrying a lot about this problem.
'Personally it worries me, very much at the moment - what was our mistake with masculinity?' he said.
“What have we taught men as they grew up in terms of the problem we observe now?”
Kit believes that television could be partially responsible for increasing toxic masculinity thanks to its extremely masculine characters.
He continued: 'What's innate and what is taught?
What is taught on TV, and in the streets, that makes kids think they have to be in this particular way of being a man?
I think this is one of the great dilemmas of our time - how can we change things?
'Because of course something went wrong for young people.'
During the course of the interview he also admitted that he will not play any prequel or sequel to Game of Thrones once the series ended next summer, stating that he “has finished with battlefields and horses.”
Starting in November, Kit will play the leading role in Sam Shepard's True West revival, the story of a film producer and his brother, who is a robber.
The actor recently revealed that he considers the meeting with his wife Rose the best thing happened with Game of Thrones.
"I met my wife during this series, so she gave me in this sense my future family, and my life from now on," she explained.
Rose played Ygritte, the object of Jon Snow’s love, Kit’s character in the Emmy-winning fantasy series.
The couple got married in June 2018 at the Lesle family estate in Scotland.
HIV/Aids: China reports 14% increase in new cases
China has announced an increase of 14% in the number of citizens suffering from HIV and AIDS.
Over 820,000 people are suffering from these diseases, according to sources of the health authorities.
About 40,000 new cases were reported as early as the second trimester
The vast majority of new cases are due to sexual transmission, which marks a change from the past.
In some regions of China HIV has spread in the past as a result of infected blood transfusions.
But the number of people who contracted HIV in this way shranked to almost zero, Chinese health authorities said at a conference in Yunnan province.
Year after year, however, the number of people suffering from HIV and Aids in China has increased by 100,000.
Transmission of HIV by sex is an urgent issue in the Chinese LGBT community.
Homosexuality was decriminalized in China in 1997, but discrimination against LGBT people is still widespread.
Due to the country's traditional values, some studies have estimated that 70-90% of men who have sex with other men eventually marry women.
Many of the transmissions of diseases are caused by inadequate protections during sexual intercourse within such relationships.
In 2003, the Chinese government promised universal access to anti-HIV drugs as part of its commitment to managing the problem.
Maxine Waters denies that a staff member leaked data from GOP senators, and talks about “dangerous lies” and “conspiracy theories.”
The U.S. congresswoman denounced on Saturday's accusations against a member of his staff regarding the publication of personal information of three U.S. republican senators in the legislators' Wikipedia pages.
The Democratic congresswoman in Los Angeles said the statements were fomented by opinion-makers and websites of the “ultra-right wing.”
“Lies, lies, and still despicable lies,” Waters said in a statement on Twitter.
The published information would include the home addresses and phone numbers of U.S. Senators Lindsey Graham of South Carolina and Mike Lee and Orrin Hatch, both from Utah.
The information was posted online on Thursday, released by an anonymous from the Capitol during a hearing of a committee of experts on allegations of sexual assault against Supreme Court nominee Brett Kavanaugh.
The leakage came after the three senators questioned Kavanaugh.
Conservative sites such as Gateway Pundit and RedState reported that the IP address identifying the source of the posts was associated with Waters’ office and released information relating to a Waters staff member.
“This unfounded accusation is completely false and an absolute lie,” Waters continued.
"The member of my staff, whose identity, personal information and security were jeopardized as a result of these misunderstood and tendentious allegations, was in no way responsible for leaking such information.
This unfounded accusation is totally false and an absolute lie.”
Waters' claim quickly spawned online criticism from former White House press secretary Ari Fleischer.
"This denial is furious," Fleischer wrote.
"This implies that it does not have the character to be a Member of Congress.
When someone is accused of something they didn't do, they don't have to get angry.
It doesn't have to be provocative.
It must not call into question the reasons of the accuser.
He must remain calm and serene."
Fleischer appeared to compare Waters' reaction to criticism of the Democrats to Judge Kavanaugh, who had been accused by detractors of appearing too angry during Thursday's hearing.
Omar Navarro, a republican candidate running to dismiss Waters in the midterm elections, expressed his views on Twitter.
"Huge if confirmed," he tweeted.
In her statement, Waters said that her office alerted “the competent authorities and law enforcement agencies of these tendentious claims.
“We’ll make sure that those responsible are unmasked,” he continued, “and that they have to answer for all their dangerous and harmful actions for all members of my staff.”
Review of Johnny English Strikes Again - The Spy's Parody with an Undersized Rowan Atkinson
It has now become customary to look for references to Brexit in every new film with a British twist and that seems applicable to this revival of Johnny’s action comedy, which began in 2003 and came back to life in 2011 with Johnny English - The Rebirth.
The veiled self-irony on the subject of how gross we do will be the nation's new export opportunity?
In any case, incompetent rubber-faced Johnny English and out-of-orbit eyes got his license to mess things up for the second time - and his name indicates more than anything that's a comedy creation developed for non-Anglophonic film user countries.
Obviously it’s the silly secreted agent who despite his strangling glamore claims has some Clouseau, a touch by Mr Bean and a pinch of that type who contributed with a single note to Gloria’s theme of the opening ceremony of the London 2012 Olympic Games.
It is a character based initially on the traveler and mysterious international man that Atkinson once played in Barclaycard's now-forgotten television ads, which left chaos behind him.
There are one or two nice moments in this latest Johnny English outing.
I liked Johnny English approaching the helicopter wearing a medieval armor and the propellers hurling briefly against his helm.
Atkinson’s gift to physical comedy is evident, but the humor seems quite undersized and strangely superfluous, particularly now that “serious” film brands like 007 and Mission Impossibile now offer comedy as an ingredient.
Humour seems directed to children instead of adults and for me Johnny English's crazy misadventures are not as original and targeted as Atkinson's mute movie gags when he plays Mr Bean.
The immutable topical premise now is the fact that Britain is in serious danger.
A computer hacker has infiltrated the super-secret web network of British spies, revealing the identities of all British agents in the field, with dismay of the agent in charge, an unfortunately very small role for Kevin Eldon.
It’s the straw that overflows the vase for a presumptuous and tormented prime minister, who is already suffering a meltdown of political unpopularity: Emma Thompson does her best with this Teresa May-like character but there isn’t much to work on in the script.
Her intelligence advisers inform her that every single active spy has been compromised and that she will have to recall someone who has retired.
And this means that the clumsy Johnny English, who now works as a school executive in a chic establishment, but who also gives lectures on how to be an undercover agent: here you can appreciate some nice gags, when English offers a School of Rock-type espionage course.
English is being knocked back at Whitehall for an emergency briefing and rejoins with his former suffering partner Bough, played by Ben Miller again.
Bough is now married, married to a submarine commander, a high-society girl role where Vicki Pepperdine is a little wasted.
So the Batman and Robin of Disasters for Her Majesty's Secret Service are back in action, to meet Olga Kurylenko's beautiful femme fatale, Ophelia Bulletova.
Meanwhile, the prime minister is falling dangerously into the network of the charismatic technology billionaire who claims he can solve Britain's computer problems: sinister Jason Volta, played by Jake Lacy.
English and Bough begin their odyssey of ridiculous adventures: disguised as waitresses, they set fire to a French restaurant; they unleash hell by sneaking into Volta’s luxury yacht and English creates pure anarchy while trying to use a virtual reality mask to become familiar with Volta’s house.
All the scenes are certainly designed for the last sequence, but however polite and exuberant, there’s quite a bit of children’s TV throughout the story.
It's all quite modest.
And as it happened with Johnny's other films, I couldn't help but think: couldn't the British film industry assign Rowan Atkinson a role doing justice to his talent?
The Labour Party denies it's drawing up a plan for Britons to work four days a week but get paid for five
Jeremy Corbyn's Labour Party is considering a radical programme that would allow British citizens to work four days a week, despite being paid for five.
The party would like leading companies to relocate savings with the artificial intelligence (AI) revolution to workers by offering them an extra refresh day.
In doing so employees could enjoy a three-day weekend while maintaining the same pay.
Some sources said the idea would be compatible with the party's economic programme and plans to tilt the country in favour of workers.
The transition to a four-day day was endorsed by the union congress as a way for workers to take advantage of the evolving economy.
A senior member of the Labour Party told The Sunday Times: "By the end of the year, a review of the political line is expected to be announced."
“It won’t happen overnight, but a four-day working week is an aspiration in line with the party’s approach to rebalancing the economy in favor of the workers, as well as its overall industrial strategy.”
The Labour Party would not be the first to support such an idea, given that the Green Party promised a four-day working week as part of the 2017 general election campaign.
However, this aspiration does not enjoy unanimous support from the Labour Party.
“A four-day working week is not part of the party’s political line and is not currently being discussed.”
