Mr McDonnell said he was determined to regain power from “faceless executives” and “speculators” in enterprises for public services.
Shadow chancellor plans also imply that the current shareholders of water distribution companies may not recover their entire equity package, since the Labour government could implement “deductions” based on behavior considered unlawful.
It has also confirmed plans to place employees on the boards of companies and create funds with inclusive participation, in order to transfer 10% of the equity of private sector companies to employees, who end up putting annual dividends up to £500 in their pockets.
Lindsey Graham and John Kennedy tell "60 Minutes" whether the FBI investigation into Kavanaugh could make them change their minds
The FBI's investigation as part of allegations against Judge Brett Kavanaugh postponed the final ballot for his nomination to the Supreme Court by at least a week, and raises doubts whether or not the government agency's findings may affect any of the Republican senators in providing their support.
In an interview that will air Sunday, "60 Minutes" correspondent Scott Pelley asked Republican senators John Kennedy and Lindsey Graham if it's possible for the FBI to unearth something capable of causing them to change their mind.
Kennedy has appeared more open than his colleague from South Carolina.
"I mean, of course," Kennedy said.
“I said it while I was going to the hearing, I said, I spoke to Judge Kavanaugh.
I called him after what had happened, that that accusation had come off, I said, ‘Did you do it?’
It was resolute, determined, unmistakable.”
Graham's vote, however, seems carved into the rock.
"I have clear ideas about Brett Kavanaugh and they could withstand a bomb-proof accusation," he said.
“Dr. Ford, I don’t know what happened, but I know this: Brett energetically denied it,” Grahad added, referring to Christine Blasey Ford.
“And whoever you name couldn’t prove it.
It's been 36 years.
“I don’t see anything changing again.”
What is the Global Citizen Festival and has it done something to reduce poverty?
This Saturday New York will host the Global Citizen Festival, an annual music event that sees the performance of a noticeable roundup of stars and has an equally remarkable mission: to end poverty in the world.
Now in its seventh edition, the Global Citizen Festival will see tens of thousands of people flocking to Central Park’s Great Lawn not only to enjoy performances such as Janet Jackson, Cardi B and Shawn Mendes, but also raising awareness about the real purpose of the event to end extreme poverty by 2030.
The Global Citizen Festival, which began in 2012, is an extension of the Global Poverty Project, an international support group that hopes to eliminate poverty by increasing the number of people actively fighting it.
To receive a free ticket for the event (unless you were willing to pay for a VIP ticket), the audience of the concert had to carry out a series of tasks, or “actions” such as volunteering, sending emails to a world leader, making a phone call or any other meaningful way that would help raise awareness about their purpose of eliminating poverty.
But, exactly, how successful was the Global Citizen Festival now that there are 12 years left to achieve its intended goal?
Is the idea of rewarding people with a free concert really a genuine way of persuading people to call for action, or is it just another example of the so-called “clicktivism” – people who feel they’re really making a difference simply by signing an online petition or sending a tweet?
Since 2011, Global Citizen claims to have recorded more than 19 million “actions” of its supporters, who have worked for a lot of different purposes.
It declares that these actions have helped to urge world leaders to announce commitments and policies amounting to more than US$37 billion, set to affect, by 2030, the lives of more than 2.25 billion people.
In early 2018, the group cited 390 commitments and announcements arising from their actions, of which at least US$10 billion have already been shelled or used as funds.
The group estimates that so far protected-equity funds have exerted a direct impact on about 649 million people worldwide.
Some of the key commitments include The Power of Nutrition, a UK-based partnership of investors and executors committed to “helping children grow to their full potential,” with a promise to provide Rwanda with US$35 million to help eliminate malnutrition in the country after receiving more than 4,700 tweets from Global Citizens.
"With the support of the UK government, donors, national governments and Global Citizens just like you, we can get social injustice or malnutrition to become a footnote in history," The Power of Nutrition Tracey Ullman Ambassador told The Power of Nutrition Tracey Ullman during a live concert in London in April 2018.
The group also said that, after more than 5,000 actions were put in place appealing to the UK to improve the nutrition of mothers and children, the government has announced the donation of funds for a project, the Power of Nutrition, which will reach 5 million women and children via interventions in favour of nutrition.
In response to one of the FAQs on your website, who asked: “What makes you think we can put an end to extreme poverty?”
Global Citizen responded: "It's going to be a long and arduous path - sometimes we're going to fall and we're going to fail.
But, like the great civil rights and the anti-apartheid movements before us, we will succeed, because together we are stronger.
Janet Jackson, the Weekend, Shawn Mendes, Cardi B, Janelle Monáe are some of those who will perform at this year’s event in New York, which will be presented by Deborra-Lee Furness and Hugh Jackman.
U.S. could use the Navy to implement "a lockdown" and thwart Russian energy exports - Home Secretary
Washington may, “if necessary”, resort to its Navy to prevent Russian energy sources from being available on the market, including that of the Middle East, U.S. Interior Secretary Ryan Zinke, as cited by the Washington Examiner.
Zinke asserted that Russian involvement in Syria - in a particular way, where it is operating at the invitation of the legitimate government - is a pretext to explore new energy markets.
"I believe that the reason they are in the Middle Earth lies in the fact that they want to negotiate for energy just as they do in Eastern Europe, the southern hub of Europe," he said.
And, according to the officials, there are means and ways to cope with it.
"The U.S. has the possibility, thanks to the Navy, to make sure that nautical routes remain open and, if necessary, implement a naval blockade, so that their energy is not placed on the market," he declared.
Zinke addressed adventors at the event hosted by the Consumer Energy Alliance, a non-profit that calls itself the “voice of energy consumers” in the United States.
He compared Washington’s strategies to negotiations with Russia and Iran, noting that they are, in fact, the same thing.
"The economic option over Iran and Russia is, more or less, to leverage and replace fuels," he said, referring to Russia as "unversal," with an economy that depends on fossil fuels.
The statements were made because the Trump administration was involved in a mission to boost the export of its own natural liquid gases to Europe, and replace Russia, an option that for European consumers is absolutely the cheapest.
For this purpose, Trump administration officials, including U.S. President Donald Trump himself, try to convince Germany to pull off from Nord Stream 2, an “inappropriate” pipeline project that, according to Trump, has made Berlin “prisoner” in Moscow.
Moscow has evidenced several times that the Nord Stream 2, pipeline of 11 billion dollars, designed to double the capacity of the pre-existing one up to 110 billion cubic meters, is a project of a purely economic nature.
The Kremlin objects that Washington's fervent opposition to the project is driven simply by economic reasons and is an example of unfair competition.
"I believe that we share the view that energy cannot be an instrument to exert pressure and that consumers should have the ability to choose suppliers," Russian Energy Minister Aleksandr Novak said, following a meeting held in September in Moscow with Rick Perry, U.S. Energy Secretary.
U.S. stancing sparked the negative reaction from Germany, which reconfirmed its commitment as part of the project.
Germany’s flagship organization for industry, the Federation of German Industries (BDI), has required the United States to stay out of European Union energy policies and bilateral agreements between Berlin and Moscow.
"I see as a big problem the fact that a third state interferes in our energy supply," Said Dieter Kempf, head of the Federation of German Industries (BDI) following a recent meeting between German Chancellor Angela Merkel and Russian President Vladimir Putin.
Elizabeth Warren will 'seriously consider' running for president in 2020, says Massachusetts Senator
Massachusetts Senator Elizabeth Warren on Saturday said she would "seriously consider" running for president following the mid-term elections.
During a city assembly in Holyoke, Massachusetts, Warren confirmed that she contemplated the possibility of running for office.
"It's time for women to go to Washington and fix our battered government, and that involves having a woman at the top," she said.
“After November 6, I’ll seriously consider running for president.”
During the city assembly Warren said he was about President Donald Trump, stating that he was “bringing this country in the wrong direction.”
"I'm deeply concerned about what Donald Trump is doing to our democracy," he said.
Warren has been explicit in criticizing Trump and Brett Kavanaugh, whom she was appointed to the Supreme Court.
On Friday Warren wrote in a tweet: "It's natural that there's a need for an FBI investigation before the vote."
A poll released on Thursday, however, showed that a majority of Warren's own voters don't feel he is expected to run in 2020.
58 percent of "probably" voters in Massachusetts said the senator shouldn't compete, according to the Suffolk University Political Research Center/Boston Globe survey.
32 percent supported such an candidacy.
The poll evinced greater support for a candidacy by former Governor Deval Patrick, with a 38 percent in support of a potential candidacy and 48 percent opposed.
Other high-profile Democrat names that were discussed regarding a potential candidacy in 2020 include former Vice President Joe Biden and Vermont Senator Bernie Sanders.
Biden said he would make an official decision by January, as reported by the Associated Press.
