18 states and the District of Columis support the challenge for new asylum policies
Eighteen states and the District of Columbia support a legal battle against a new U.S. policy that denies asylum to victims fleeing gangs or domestic violence.
Representatives of the 18 states and the District of Justico on Friday filed an amicus curiae in Washington to support an asylum seeker disputing that policy, as reported by NBC News.
The full name of the plaintiff in the Grace v. Sessions appeal that the American Civil Liberties Union filed in August against federal policy was not revealed.
He stated that her partner "and her violent gang-member children" abused her, but U.S. officials rejected her asylum application on July 20.
She's been detained in Texas.
Prosecutors supporting Grace described El Salvador, Honduras and Guatemala, generating a large number of asylum candidates in the U.S
The U.S. New Asylum Policy overturned a decision taken in 2014 by the Board of Immigrant Appeals that allowed an asylum application to undocumented immigrants fleeing domestic violence.
