Desperate recovery attempts in Palu as the death toll doubles in the race to find survivors
For the survivors, the situation became increasingly desperate.
“There’s a lot of tension,” 35-year-old mother Risa Kusuma said, while comforting her little feverish child in an evacuation center in the destroyed city of Palu.
“At every moment there is an ambulance carrying bodies.
Cleaned water is scarce.”
Residents were seen returning to their destroyed dwellings, rummaging among their food belongings, in an attempt to recover whatever they could find.
Hundreds of people were injured and the hospitals, damaged by the magnitude 7.5 quake, were overflowing.
Some of the wounded, including Dwi Haris, the victim of a shoulder-and-back fracture, rested outside at Palu Military Hospital, where patients were treated outside due to the continuing severe aftershocks.
They filled his eyes with tears as he recounted the feeling given by the violent quake shaking the room he shared with his wife and daughter on the fifth floor of a hotel.
“There was no time to save us.
