I don't know what happened to you and my daughter.
I hope they’re safe.”
U.S. ambassador accuses China of 'making bullies' with 'advertising propaganda'
A week after an official Chinese newspaper published a four-page advertisement in a U.S. newspaper, publicizing U.S.-China trade benefits, the U.S. ambassador to China accused Beijing of using the U.S
U.S. On Wednesday, President Donald Trump referred to the announcement paid by the China Daily in the Des Moines Register, the newspaper with the highest sales in the state of Iowa, after accusing China of trying to interfere in the U.S. congressional elections on November 6, an accusation that China rejects.
Trump’s accusation that Beijing was trying to meddle in the U.S. election, remarked what U.S. officials told Reuters was a new phase of an escalation campaign by Washington to pressure China.
While for foreign governments it is normal to advertise to promote trade, Beijing and Washington are currently involved in an escalating trade war that has seen them adapt their duties to their respective imports.
China's retaliatory tariffs at the start of the trade war had been thought to target exporters to states like Iowa, which supported Trump's Republican Party, as Chinese and U.S. experts said.
Terry Branstad, U.S. ambassador to China and former long-time governor of Iowa, a major exporter of agricultural goods in China, said Beijing had harmed U.S. workers, farmers and businesses.
China, Branstad wrote in an article of opinion in Sunday’s Des Moines Register, “now increases those bullies by publishing advertising propaganda in our free newspapers.”
"In disseminating its own propaganda, the Chinese government is using the beloved American tradition of freedom of speech and the press, placing a paid advertisement in the Des Moines Register," Branstad wrote.
"Conversely, in the newsstand on the road here in Beijing you will find little dissident voices and you will not see any real reflection of the disparate views that the Chinese population might have on China's problematic economic trajectory, given that the media are under the firm fist of the Chinese Communist Party," he wrote.
He added that "one of China's most distinguished newspapers evaded an offer to publish" his article, although he did not state which newspaper it is.
The Republicans are alienating the female electorate before the mid-term elections with the Kavanaugh case, analysts warn
Given that many leading Republicans defend Brett Kavanaugh's nomination to the Supreme Court and are on his side in dealing with various sexual harassment allegations, analysts have signaled that a negative reaction will be seen, especially by women, over the course of future midterm elections.
Agitation in this regard has been extremely high, and many Republicans have already officially shown that they want to continue with a vote.
"You can't withdraw these things," he told The Hill Grant Reeher, a professor of political science at Syracuse University's Maxwell School, for an article published on Saturday.
Reeher said he doubted Senator Jeff Flake (R-Arizona's last-minute insistence) for an FBI's investigation will be enough to appease angry voters.
“Women won’t forget what happened yesterday – they won’t forget it tomorrow or in November,” said Karine Jean-Pierre, Karine Jean-Pierre, a senior consultant and national spokesperson for the progressive group MoveOn.
