"The enthusiasm and motivation of the Democrats are skyrocketing," Stu Rothenberg, an independent political analyst, told the news site.
“People say they were already tall; that’s true.
“But they could be taller, especially among undecided electors in the suburbs and young voters, between the ages of 18 and 29, who often don’t vote despite not liking for the president.”
Even prior to Ford's public testimony, which provided details of his allegations of sexual harassment against the Supreme Court nominee, analysts had speculated that a negative reaction could occur if the Republicans continued with confirmation.
"For the GOP it's become a confusing set," Michael Steele, a former president of the Republican National Committee, said earlier last week, according to NBC News.
“We’re not just talking about the vote for the committee, it’s about the final vote or whether Kavanaugh will be on the bench, it’s also about how the Republicans handled the situation and the way they treated it,” Guy Cecil, director of Priorities U.S
In any case, the Americans seem to be somewhat divided among the person to believe as a result of Ford and Kavanaugh’s testimonies, with a lower percentage in favor of the latter.
