Ramirez alleges that the Supreme Court nominee exposed her genitals during a party during her studies at Yale in the 1980s.
The inventor of the World Wide Web plans to start a New Internet to challenge Google and Facebook
Tim Berners-Lee, inventor of the World Wide Web, is launching a startup trying to rival Facebook, Amazon and Google.
The latest technology legend project, Inrupt, is a company that originates from Berners-Lee’s open source Solid platform.
Solid allows users to choose where to store their data and what people will have access to and what information.
In an exclusive interview with Fast Company, Berners-Lee joked that the intent behind Inrupt is “dominating the world.”
“We have to do it now,” he said.
“It’s a historic moment.”
The app uses Solid’s technology to allow people to create their own “online personal data store” or POD.
It can include lists of contacts, things to do, calendars, music libraries, and other personal and professional instruments.
It’s as if Google Drive, Microsoft Outlook, Slack and Spotify are all available on one browser and at the same time.
The uniqueness of the online personal data warehouse is that it is completely the user’s choice who can have access to what type of information.
The company calls it “personal empowerment through data.”
The idea for Inrupt, according to John Bruce, CEO of the company, is to provide adequate resources, processes and skills to make Solid accessible to everyone.
The company currently consists of Berners-Lee, Bruce, a security platform bought by IBM, some developers on the staff hired to work on the project, and a community of volunteer programmers.
Starting this week, technology programmers around the world will be able to create their decentralized apps using the tools available on the Inrupt website.
Berners-Lee said he and his team won’t tell “Facebook and Google whether or not to introduce a full change where their business models are fully beaten overnight.
“We’re not asking their permission.”
“Inrupt’s mission is to provide commercial energy and an ecosystem that helps protect the integrity and quality of the new web built from Solid.”
In 1994 Berners-Lee transformed the internet when he founded the World Wide Web Consortium at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology.
Berners-Lee has been an influential voice in the net neutrality debate in recent months.
Even after Inrupt’s launch, Berners-Lee will remain the Founder and Manager of the World Wide Web Consortium, the Web Foundation and the Open Data Institute.
"I'm incredibly optimistic about this new era of the web," Berners-Lee added.
Bernard Vann: Commemorate the clergyman of the Great War with Victoria Cross
The Church of England clergymen alone to be rewarded with a Victoria Cross during the First World War as a fighter was commemorated in her hometown after 100 years.
Lieutenant Colonel, Reverend Bernard Vann, was recognized on September 29, 1918 in the attack on Bellenglise and Lehaucourt.
However, four days later he was killed by a sniper and never knew that he had achieved the highest level of British military honor.
On Saturday at a parade in Rushden, Northamptonshire, her two grandchildren ushered in a memorial slab.
One of his grandchildren, Michael Vann, said it was "magnificantly symbolic" the fact that the stone would be inaugurated exactly 100 years after his grandfather's award-winning prowess.
According to the London Gazette, on September 29/198 Lieutenant Colonel Van led his battalion beyond the Saint-Quentin Canal “throught through a dense fog, under the heavy fire of the camp and war machines.”
Then he hurried towards the line of fire and with the “greatest gallantery” advanced the line before running with a single-hand cannon and landing three of the detachment.
Lieutenant Colonel Vann was killed by a German sniper on October 4, 1918 - just a month before the end of the war.
Michael Vann, 72, said his grandfather’s actions were “something I know I can never live up to and something that makes you more humble.”
He and his brother, Dr. James Vann, also laid a wreath of flowers after the parade, conducted by the Brentwood Imperial Youth Band.
Michael Vann said that “he really felt honoured to play a role in the parade” and added “the value of a true hero is demonstrated by the supportunity that will be given to him by so many people.”
MMA fans stayed up all night to watch Bellator 206, and instead they got caught Peppa Pig
Imagine a little, you stayed up all night to watch the famous Bellator 206 only to see you denied the vision of the main event.
The programme from San Jose included 13 fights, including the six on the main card, and was to be broadcast in the UK overnight, live on Channel 5.
At 6 a.m., just when Gegard Mousasi and Rory MacDonald were preparing to clash, viewers in the UK were sturrent when Peppa Pig appeared on screen.
Some remained based after staying awake until the early hours of the morning specifically for the fight.
On Twitter a fan described the transition to the children's cartoon as "a kind of bad-tasting joke."
"Government's regulation stipulates that at six o'clock in the morning that content wasn't more appropriate, so you've had to switch to children's programming," said Dave Schwartz, Bellator's senior vice president for marketing and communication, when asked about the broadcast.
“Peppa the pig”, “yes.”
“Bellator president Scott Coker has stated that they will work on programming to include viewers from the UK in the future.
"I believe that when I think about replay, I think we can probably come up with a solution," Coker said.
“But there it’s six o’clock in the morning on Sunday and we won’t be able to find a solution before Sunday of our time zone on Monday.
But we're working on it.
Believe me, a lot of messages arrived when it changed schedule and no one was friendly.
We were trying to solve the problem, we thought it was a technical problem.
But he wasn't, it was a government problem.
I promise it won’t happen next time.
We're going to limit it to five fights instead of six - as we normally do - and we've tried to get so much for fans, and we've just discussed it.
It’s an unpleasant situation.”
Desert Island Discs: Tom Daley felt 'inferior' due to sexuality
Olympic swimmer Tom Daley says he grew up feeling inferior to anyone because of his own sexuality - but that gave him the motivation to succeed.
The 24-year-old said he didn’t realize, until he got to secondary school, that “not everyone is like me.”
Speaking in the first installment of Radio 4's Desert Island Discs, Lauren Laverne said she had made her voice heard about gay rights to give "hope" to others.
He also stated that having become a parent caused him to care less to win the Olympics.
The usual presenter of the long-standing program, Kirsty Young, took a series of months off for illness.
Presenting as an outcast in Laverne’s first program, Daley said he felt “less than” anyone else while growing up because “it wasn’t socially acceptable that he liked both boys and girls.”
She said: "Nowadays, the feeling of feeling less than, and of feeling different, were the things that really gave me the strength and energy to succeed."
He wanted to try to be “something,” he said, so he wouldn’t let anyone down if they happened to find out about his sexuality.
The two-time bronze medalist at the Olympics became a high-profile LGBT activist and used his face in this year's Australian Commonwealth Games to appeal to multiple countries to decriminalize homosexuality.
He said he had made his voice heard because he felt lucky to be able to live openly without consequences and wanted to give “hope” to others.
The three-time world champion said the fact of falling in love with a man - U.S. director Dustin Lance Black, whom he met in 2013 - "caught me by surprise."
Daley married the Oscar winner, who is 20 years older than him, last year, but said the age difference was never a problem.
“When you spend so much of it at a young age,” he went to his early Olympics when he was 14 and his father died of cancer three years later, he said it was hard to find someone of the same age who had experienced similar ups and downs.
The two became parents in June, of a child named Robert Ray Black-Daley, and Daley said his "entire perspective" changed.
“If I asked myself last year, it was all about the fact that ‘I had to win a gold medal,’” he said.
“You know, there are more important things than Olympic gold medals.
“My Olympic gold medalist is Robbie.”
His son bears the same name as his father Robert, who died in 2011 at the age of 40 after he was diagnosed with brain cancer.
Daley said that her father hadn't agreed to be about to die and that one of the last things she asked was if they already had tickets to London 2012 - because she wanted to be in the front row.
“I couldn’t tell him ‘you won’t be around here so you can be in the front row, Dad,’” he said.
"I was holding his hand when he stopped breathing and until he actually quit and died, I hadn't realised he wasn't invincible yet," he said.
The following year Daley competed in the 2012 Olympics, winning bronze.
"I just knew it was what I'd dreamed of all my life - swimming in front of an audience at home in the Olympic Games, there was no better feeling," he said.
This also inspired the choice of his first song - Heather Small's Proud - which had particular significance for him in preparing for the Olympics and still gives him goosebumps.
Desert Island Discs is on BBC Radio 4 on Sunday at 11:15 a.m
Mickelson out-of-form put on the bench in Saturday's Ryder Cup
American Phil Mickelson will score a record on Sunday when he plays his 47th Ryder Cup match, but he will have to get back in shape to prevent it from becoming an unhappy milestone.
Mickelson, playing in the two-year event for a record-breaking twelfth, was put on the bench by captain Jim Furyk for Saturday's fourballs and foursomes.
Instead of being at the heart of the action, as it was often for the United States, the five-time champion spent the day between cheerleading and working on one’s own game in the range, in the hope of correcting what torments him.
Having never been the strongest of drivers even at the pinnacle of his career, the 48-year-old is not the ideal choice for the difficult Le Golf National, where the disjointed course regularly punishes the wrong shots.
And if the path in itself wasn’t daunting enough, Mickelson, in Sunday’s ninth-match, faces careful British Open champion Francesco Molinari, who teamed up with novice Tommy Fleetwood to win all four of their matches this week.
If the Americans, four points below at the start of their 12 singles matches, start with a blistering start, Mickelson's match could prove absolutely crucial.
Furyk expressed confidence in his own man, not that he could say much more.
“He fully understood the role she had today, gave me a pat on the shoulder, put an arm around me and said he’ll be ready for tomorrow,” Furyk recounted.
“He has a lot of self-confidence.
It's a Hall of Famer and he's added so much to teams in the past, and this week.
I probably didn’t imagine him playing two games.
I had imagined more, but this is the way it went and that's the way we'd thought it should go.
He wants to participate, just like anyone else.”
Mickelson on Sunday will surpass Nick Faldo's record for the most games played in the Ryder Cup.
It could mark the end of a career in the Ryder Cup that has never even remotely mirrored the heights of his personal record.
Mickelson has 18 games won, 20 defeats and seven draws, although Furyk has said his presence has brought intangible assets to the team.
"He's funny, sarcastic, witty, he likes to make fun of people, and he's an amazing guy to have in the boardroom," he explained.
"I also believe that the younger players enjoyed grapping against him this week, something that was fun to watch.
He did so much more than playing.”
The captain of Europe, Thomas Bjorn, knows that a good advantage may fade soon
Thomas Bjorn, the captain of Europe, knows from experience that a considerable advantage in the individual encounters of the last day of the Ryder Cup can easily turn into an uncomfortable run-up.
The Danish debuted in the 1997 meeting in Valderrama in which the team, led by Seve Ballesteros, despite a five-point gap from the Americans, arrived at the finish line with a minimal advantage, winning 141⁄2-131⁄2.
"I keep telling myself that we had a good advantage in Valderrama, we had it in Brookline, where we lost, and in Valderrama where we won, but by a little," Bjorn (pictured) said after seeing the 2018 class win, Friday by 5 to 3 and yesterday by 10 to 6, at Le Golf National.
“Therefore it will be the story to prove to me and my team that it’s not over.
Tomorrow we have to give our best.
You have to go out there and do everything right.
It's not over until the stitches are scored on the whiteboard.
Our goal is to try to win the trophy, and that's what we need to focus on.
I’m always focusing on the 12 players on my team, but I know who’s on the other side: the best golfers in the world.”
Happy with the way his people competed in that difficult golf course, Bjorn added: “I would never have gotten over it.
Tomorrow is a different challenge.
Tomorrow is the turn of individual performances, which is something else.
It's great to be there with a teammate when things go well, but when you're just your golfing skills they're put to the test.
This is the message you have to convey to the players: tomorrow, you have to bring out the best of you.
Then, you forget about your mate because he also has to go there and bring out the best of himself.”
Unlike Bjorn, counterpart Jim Furyk will look in his players for the best individual more than team performance, with the exception of Jordan Spieth and Justin Thomas who collected three points out of four.
Furyk himself found himself on both sides in those great meetings of the last day, having been part of the winning team at Brookline before losing against the European “Miracle of Medinah”.
“I remember every single word,” he commented in response to the question of how Ben Crenshaw, captain in 1999, led his players into the lead in the final day.
“Tomorrow we have 12 important games, but we want to start immediately on the right foot like we did Brookline and Medinah.
When the match starts to take a certain turn, the pressure increases in intermediate matches.
So we organize our training accordingly and classify the boys in the way it seems appropriate, because tomorrow we will try to do magic.
Thomas was given the task of leading the counteroffensive and facing Rory McIlroy in the top match with, in order, the other Europeans, Paul Casey, Justin Rose, Jon Rahm, Tommy Fleetwood and Ian Poulter, in the first half.
“I chose this order because I think it covers the path to the end,” Bjorn said about his selection for individual meetings.
Germany's new military ship has been postponed again
According to local media, the last frigate of the German Navy was supposed to be commissioned in 2014 to replace the old military ships of the times of the Cold War, but there won't be at least until next year due to faulty systems and a surge in costs.
Rheinland-Pfalz's commission, the flagship of the new Baden-Württemberg-class frigates, was postponed until the first half of 2019, according to the Die Zeit newspaper citing a military spokesman.
The ship was supposed to join the Navy in 2014, but worrying issues following the handover tormented the fate of the ambitious project.
The four Baden-Württemberg-class ships ordained by the Navy in 2007 will replace the old ships of the Bremen class.
They will be equipped with a powerful cannon, a range of anti-aircraft and anti-ship missiles as well as some hidden technologies, such as reduced radar, infrared and acoustic markings.
Other important characteristics include longer maintenance periods, in fact it should be possible to use the new frigates up to two years away from national ports.
However, the continuous delays imply that the innovative warships, which are said to allow Germany to project their overseas power, will already become obsolete when they enter service, the Die Zeit points out.
The unlucky frigate Tipo F-125 made headlines last year, when the German Navy officially refused to commission the ship and returned it to Hamburg’s Blohm & Voss shipyard.
It was the first time the Navy had returned a ship to the builder after delivery.
Little has been known about the reasons for the return, but the German media have noticed a number of crucial “software and hardware defects” that have rendered the ship useless if employed on a combat mission.
Deficiencies in the software were particularly important as Baden-Württemberg-class ships will be operated by a crew of about 120 sailors, just half of the staff of the oldest ships in the Bremen class.
Moreover, it has emerged that the ship weighs far too much, and this reduces its performance and limits the Navy's ability to add future upgrades.
Rheinland-Pfalz is believed to be twice as heavy as ships of similar classes used by the Germans in World War II.
Apart from faulty hardware, even the cost of the entire project (including crew training) is becoming an important issue.
It seems to have reached 3.1 billion euros (3.6 billion dollars) from the initial 2.2 billion euros.
The problems involving the new frigates become of particular importance in the light of the recent alarms concerning the reduction of German naval power.
At the beginning of the year, Hans-Peter Bartels, parliamentary commissioner for the Armed Forces, admitted that the Navy actually "scarce of ships capable of being employed."
The official stated that the issue has deteriorated markedly over time, since the old unarmed ships have not been replaced by new ships.
His regret is that none of Baden-Württemberg's frigates were able to join the Navy.
The National Trust spies the secret life of bats
New research carried out in a Scottish Highland estate aims to reveal how bats use sight to source food.
Confides that the findings can shed light on the behavior of the only existing flying mammals and drive future guardianship activities.
The study of scientists from the National Trust for Scotland will follow common and soprano bats, in addition to Daubenton's brown mumps and bats at Inverewe Gardens in Wester Ross.
Special recorders will be placed in key locations around the property to trace bat activities during the season.
NHS staff and volunteers will also conduct mobile surveys using portable detectors.
Analysis of recorded sounds by an expert will determine the frequency of bat recalls and how each species behaves.
There will then be produced a map of the habitat and a report to create a detailed scale picture of their behavior.
Rob Dewar, nature conservation consultant for NTS, hopes the results can reveal which areas of the habitat are most important for bats and how these are used by each species.
This information will help determine the benefits of habitat management jobs, such as the creation of meadows, and how to best maintain forests for bats and other related species.
Bat population in Scotland and the United Kingdom has declined sharply over the last century.
Bats are at risk due to construction and development work affecting trespoles and ruinizing the habitat.
Wind turbines and lighting can also be a threat, as well as musicide paper and some chemical treatments of building materials, in addition to the attacks of domestic cats.
Bats aren't really blind.
However, due to their night hunting habits, their ears are more useful than eyes when it comes to grabbing a prey.
They use a sophisticated eco-location technique to detect insects and obstacles on their trajectory.
The NTS, responsible for caring for more than 270 historic buildings, 38 important gardens and 76,000 hectares of land in the country, takes bats very seriously.
Ten qualified experts regularly conduct investigations, inspect trespols and sometimes carry out bailouts.
The organisation has even established Scotland's first reserve intended solely for bats at the Threave estate in the Dumfries and Galloway region, home to eight of Scotland's ten bat species.
The estate manager, David Thompson, says the property is the ideal territory.
“Here at Threave we have a large area for bats,” he said.
“We have the ancient buildings, many centuries-old trees and good habitat.
But there’s a lot about bats we don’t know yet, so the work we’re doing here and in the other properties will help us understand more about their growth needs.”
It emphasizes the importance of controlling bats before conducting maintenance within properties as it is possible that the involuntary destruction of a single maternal trespole can kill up to 400 females and babies, perhaps wiping out an entire local population.
Bats are protected and it is illegal to kill them, harass them, disturb them or destroy their trespolines.
Elisabeth Ferrell, Scottish manager for the Bat Conservation Trust, encouraged the public to help help.
He said: “We still have a lot to learn about our bats and for many of our species we don’t know how their populations pass it.”
Ronaldo dismisses rape allegations while lawyers sue German weekly
Cristiano Ronaldo labelled the allegations of rape against him as "fake news," stating that people "want to advertise themselves" by his name.
His lawyers are preparing to sue the German weekly Der Spiegel that published the allegations.
The Portuguese Juventus striker was accused of raping an American woman named Kathryn Mayorga, at a Las Vegas hotel in 2009.
Der Spiegel also announced on Friday the accusation of the payment of 375,000 dollars to keep silence on what happened.
In an Instagram video directed at his 142 million followers, in the hours following the allegations, Ronaldo, 33, nipped statements as "fake news."
“No, no, no, no, no.
What they said today is untrue," says the five-time Ballon d'Or facing the camera.
“They want to advertise through my name.
That's normal.
They want to be famous by making my name, but that's part of the game.
I'm happy and everything's fine," the footballer added, smiling.
According to Reuters, Ronaldo’s lawyers are preparing to sue Der Spiegel about the allegations, called “an inadmissible reporting of suspects on the private sphere.”
Lawyer Christian Schertz said the footballer will seek compensation for "moral damage to the extent corresponding to the seriousness of the violation of privacy, considered, probably, one of the most serious of personal rights in recent years."
The fact would take place in June 2009 in a suite at the Palms Casino Resort in Las Vegas.
After meeting at a nightclub, Ronaldo and Mayorga returned to the footballer’s room, where he subjected her to anal violation, according to the statements filed with the Clark County District Court in Nevada.
Mayorga argues that Ronaldo allegedly got to his knees after the incident by saying that he is “99 percent” a “great guy” disappointed by “one percent.”
From the documents it emerges that Ronaldo confirmed that the two had sexual intercourse, but that this was consensual.
Mayorga also declares that she went to the police and the hospital, where her injuries were photographed, but had later agreed to a conciliation agreement because she felt “terrified of retaliation” and worried by the prospect of being “publicly humiliated”.
The 34-year-old now tries to overturn the deal, claiming she is stilly traumatized by the alleged incident.
Ronaldo was on the verge of switching to Real Madrid from Manchester United at the time of what happened, and this summer he will join Italian Juventus with a 100-million-euro contract.
Brexit: U.K. will forever regret the loss of car manufacturers
The UK "could regret forever" the loss of its status as a world leader in car-building in the aftermath of Brexit, Industry Minister Greg Clark said.
He also added that he was "concerned" about Toyota UK's statements to the BBC about Britain's eventual exit from the EU without a deal and the ensuing momentary blocking of production at the Burnaston factory, near Derby.
“We need an agreement,” Clark said.
The Japanese carmaker argues that border delays, in the event of a no-deal Brexit, could affect jobs.
Burnaston’s factory, where the Toyota Auris and Avensis are built, produced around 150,000 cars last year, of which 90% were exported to the rest of the European Union.
"I believe that if Britain leaves the EU at the end of March, production in our factory will stop," said Marvin Cooke, Toyota's Managing Director in Burnaston.
It heightens concern in other British car manufacturers about leaving the EU without an agreement on the functioning of cross-border trade, including Honda, BMW and Jaguar Land Rover.
BMW, for example, argues that after Brexit it will close its Mini plants in Oxford for a month.
The main concern relates to risks in the supply chain found by manufacturers in the event of no-deal Brexit.
Toyota’s production line is managed on the basis of the “just in time” concept, with parts arriving every 37 minutes from British and European suppliers for custom cars.
If the United Kingdom leaves the EU without an agreement on 29 March, inconvenience at the borders that would cause delays and shortages of parties.
It would be impossible for Toyota to keep more than a day worth of inventory at its plant in Derbyshire, the company said, so production would be stopped.
Minister Clark argues that Theresa May's plan for future relations with the EU is "thought with precision to avoid these inconveniences at the borders."
“We need an agreement. We want to have the best deal that will allow us to enjoy not only current success but seize this opportunity,” he told BBC Radio 4.
“The proof, not only from Toyota but also from other car manufacturers, is that we absolutely have to be able to continue what has been a very successful supply chain.”
Toyota hasn't been able to say how long production could stop but, in the long run, it warns that additional costs could reduce plant competitiveness and potentially jobs.
Peter Tsouvallaris, who worked in Burnaston for 24 years and is in charge of the plant’s trade union delegates, declares that the workers are deeply concerned: “Based on my experience, once jobs are reduced, they are no longer reinstated.”
“We have proposed a precise and credible plan for future relations with the EU.”
Trump's meeting with Rosenstein could be postponed again, the White House said.
Donald Trump's high-risk meeting with Deputy Justice Minister Rod Rosenstein could be "postponed by a week," while the dispute over Judge Brett Kavanaugh continues, the White House said on Sunday.
Rosenstein oversees the work of special prosecutor Robert Mueller investigating Russian interference in the election, the links between Trump's advisers and Russia, and the eventual hurdle to justice by the president.
The possibility that Trump could fire the Deputy Minister of Justice, thus jeopardizing Mueller's independence, has fueled Washington gossip for months.
Over the course of the month, The New York Times reported that Rosenstein was talking about wearing a bug to record conversations with Trump and trying to remove the president through the 25th Amendment.
Rosenstein has denied the news.
But last Monday he went to the White House and rumoured he was about to resign.
Instead, it was announced for Wednesday a meeting with Trump, which stood at that time at the United Nations in New York.
Trump said he “would rather not” fire Rosenstein, but then the meeting was postponed to avoid an overlap with the Senate Judiciary Committee hearing in which Kavanaugh and one of the women who accused him of sexual assault, Dr. Christine Blasey Ford, both testified.
On Friday, Trump ordered a week's inquiry of applications by the FBI against Kavanaugh, further delaying the Senate's final vote.
On Sunday, Trump's spokeswoman, Sarah Sanders, appeared on Fox News.
When asked about the meeting with Rosenstein, she said: "It hasn't been set a date, it could be this week, I guess she'll postpone another week considering everything that's going on with the Supreme Court.
“But we will see and continue to update the press.”
Some reporters could dispute this claim: Sanders hasn't held a press briefing in the White House since September 10.
Host Chris Wallace asked for the reason.
Sanders said the scarcity of briefingings was not due to an aversion to television reporters "who put themselves on display," although he said, "I don't disagree with the fact that they show off."
He therefore suggested that direct contact between Trump and the press could be more frequent.
“The president holds more questions and answer sessions than any president before him,” he said, adding without citing evidence: “We’ve checked the figures.”
The briefings will still be there, Sanders said, but “if the press has the opportunity to ask questions directly to the president of the United States, it’s much better than talking to me.
Let’s try to do that, as in recent weeks, and this will replace briefings with the press and you can talk to the president of the United States.”
Trump regularly answers questions when he leaves the White House or participates in open sessions or press conferences with visiting dignitaries.
Press conferences are rare.
This week in New York the president perhaps proved the reason, making a free-wheeling speech, and at the same time bizarre, in front of a group of journalists.
The Health Minister writes to European workers at the Scottish NHS to express his concerns about Brexit
The Health Minister wrote to EU staff working at Scotland's NHS to express the country's gratitude and wish them to stay after Brexit.
Scottish Parliament MP Jeane Freeman has sent a letter less than six months in advance of the UK's exit from the EU.
The Scottish Government has already pledged to meet the costs of settled status applications for EU citizens working in decentralised public services.
In his letter, Minister Freeman writes: 'Throughout the summer there have been ongoing negotiations between the UK and the EU on Brexit, which will lead to autumn decisions.
But the British government has also prepared itself in the eventuality of a no-deal scenario.
I know that this is a time of great concern for all of you.
This is why I wanted to reiterate today my appreciation for the contribution made by all members of staff, regardless of nationality.
Colleagues from across the EU, and beyond, bring valuable experience and expertise that strengthens and enhances the work of health services, giving benefits to the patients and communities to whom we offer service.
“Scotland is certainly your home, and we strongly wish that you stay here.”
Christion Abercrombie was operated on urgently following a head injury
Tennessee-State Tigers defender Christion Abercrombie underwent an emergency surgery after reporting a head injury in Saturday's game against the Vanderbilt Commodores in which the team lost 31 to 27, as Mike Organ of The Tennessean newspaper reported.
Technical manager Rod Reed told reporters that the accident that caused the injury occurred shortly before halftime.
"He approached on the sidelines and passed out," Reed said.
First aid workers and medical staff gave Abercrombie oxygen on the sidelines, before putting him on a stretcher and taking him away for further investigation.
A Tennessee State executive told Chris Harris of the WSMV in Nashville, Tennessee that Abercrombie underwent surgery at Vanderbilt University Medical Center.
Harris added that "you still don't have news about the type and extent of the damage" and that the Tennessee State are trying to figure out when he was injured.
Abercrombie, a sophomore student, is in his first season with the Tennessee State after moving in from the Illinois.
He had carried out a total of five tackles before abandoning the match, which brought his season total to 18 tackles.
Foreign buyers will be subject to higher stamp duties for the purchase of properties in the UK
Foreign buyers will be subject to higher stamp duties to purchase properties in the UK and additional cash flow will be used to aid the homeless, according to Labour's new plans
The maneuver will neutralise Corbyn's success of attracting young voters
The stamp duty increase will be applied to taxpayers who do not pay taxes in the UK
The Treasury expects to raise up to £120m a year to help the homeless
Foreign buyers will be subject to higher stamp duties to purchase properties in the UK and additional cash flow will be used to help the homeless, Theresa May announced today.
The manoeuvre will be seen as an attempt to neutralise the success of Jeremy Corbyn's initiative to attract young voters with the promise of providing more affordable housing and higher incomes.
The increase in stamp duty will be applied to individuals and non-tax-paying companies in the United Kingdom, and additional resources will be in support of the Government’s initiative to combat the homelessness phenomenon.
The surcharge, which adds to the current stamp duty, including the high levels introduced two years ago on second homes and buy-to-let properties (bought to be rented), could make up more than 3%.
The Treasury predicts that the manoeuvre could raise up to 120 million pounds a year.
An estimated 13% of new construction in London are being bought by non-residing nationals in the UK, raising prices and making it difficult for first-house buyers to enter the housing market.
Many of the country’s wealthiest areas, especially in the capital, have become “ghost towns” due to the large number of foreign buyers who spend most of their time outside the state.
The new policy comes just weeks after Boris Johnson had requested a stamp duty cut to help the youngest by buying the first home.
He blamed large-scale construction firms to keep property prices high by grabbing land without using them and encouraged Prime Minister May to waive quotas on affordable housing to fix Britain's "real estate misfortune."
MP Corbyn has announced an egregious set of proposals to reform the housing market, including controls on rents and the elimination of evictions without guilt.
He also wants to give the council greater powers over the construction of new properties.
Prime Minister May said: "Last year I said that I would be committed to re-establishing the British dream, namely that life should be better for the new generations.
And that means restoring the housing market.
Britain will always be open to people who want to live, work and build a life here.
However, it wouldn’t be fair if buying a house was equally easy for people who don’t reside in the UK and for foreign companies, as it is for resident British workers.
For so many people the dream of owning a house has become too distant and the dignity of homelessness remains a reality.”
Jack Ross: “My maximum aspiration is to lead Scotland”
Sunderland Technical Director Jack Ross says his “maximum aspiration” is to sooner or later become Scotland’s manager.
The Scot, 42, is attracted by the challenge of raising the North-East team which is currently in third place in the League One, three points from the top.
He landed at the Stadium of Light this summer after leading St. Mirren during last season's Scottish Premiership.
“I wanted to play for my country as a footballer.
I've been in Serie B and that's it," Ross told the Scottish BB Sportsound programme.
I grew up watching Scotland in Hampden with my father as a kid and it's something I'll always remember.
However, this will only be possible if I am successful as the manager of a team.”
Ross' predecessors at the helm of Sunderland were Dick Advocaat, David Moyes, Sam Allardyce, Martin O'Neill, Roy Keane, Gus Poyet and Paulo Di Canio.
Former Alloa coach Athletic claims he felt no anxiety in following such important names, in an equally important team, having previously rejected Barnsley and Ipswich Town proposals.
“Success for me at the moment is measured by the ability to bring the team back to the Premier League.”
Considering the structure and facilities of the club, we can say that it undoubtedly belongs to the Premier League," he said.
“It’s not an easy task, but I think I’ll only be successful if I can get the team up.”
Ross’s career start as a manager dates back three years, after a period as an assistant coach in Dumbarton and 15 months as a self-reflected Hearts coach.
He then helped Alloa save himself from relegation to the third level and took St. Mirren from the brink of relegation to the title of champions of the Championship, the following season.
Ross claims to feel much more comfortable now than during his career as a footballer in the Clyde, Hartlepool, Falkirk, St. Mirren and Hamilton Academical teams.
“It was probably a real turning point,” he recalls about the direction of the Alloa.
“I really believed that the role of manager was made just for me, rather than that of a player.
It seems strange because I have worked well, I have gained a living and I have had some good results.
But playing can be tough.
There are many things you have to deal with weekly.
I still address them in terms of stress and work pressure but the management looks good.
“I’ve always wanted to direct, and now I’m doing it, I feel much quieter than I’ve ever been.”
You can hear the full interview on Sportsound on Saturday, September 30, on Radio Scotland between 12:00 and 13:00 BST
