The summer heatwave has brought greater revenues to struggling British pubs and more concern about catering services.
Data show that pubs and bars saw a 2.7 percent increase in sales in July, while restaurant receipts dropped 4.8 percent.
Peter Martin, of the commercial consulting firm CGA, put the data together and commented: "The persistance of the sun and England's long stay at the World Cup meant that the month of July followed a route similar to that of June, when pubs were over 2.8 percent, while restaurants were hit harder.
The 1.8-percent slump in catering in June worsened in July.
Pubs and bars in which mostly drinking were the most solid, with a homogeneous increase greater than the decline in restaurants.
Even the pubs in which you eat have suffered the heat, although not excessively like restaurateurs.
It seems that people just wanted to go outside and have a drink.
In pubs and managed bars, beverage sales climbed by 6.6 percent in the month, while food consumption dropped by three percent.”
Paul Newman, RSM's recreational and receptive industry analyst, said: "These results continue the trend we've seen since the end of April.
The weather and the impact of major social or sporting events remains the main factor when it comes to selling in the retail market.
Unsurprisingly, restaurants continue to struggle, although a 4.8 percent annual sales drop will be particularly burdensome in addition to continued cost pressures.
The long hot summer could not have come in a worse period for restaurateurs and only the weather can tell if the most moderate temperatures, which we enjoyed in August, will give us a necessary truce.”
Global sales growth across pubs and restaurants, including new openings, was 2.7 percent in July, reflecting declining brand roll-outs.
Sales monitoring company Coffer Peach Tracker for British pubs, bars and restaurants puts together and analyzes performance data via 47 operating groups, with a combined turnover of more than £9bn, and is the sector's reference company.
