The advertising of a breast pump compares mothers and cows and divides online opinion
A company producing a breast pump has divided the opinion on the net with an advertisement that compares nursing mothers and cows that are milked.
To mark the launch of what is referred to as the world’s “world’s “World’s First Silent and Wearable Breasted”, consumer technology company Elvie has launched an advertisement in the form of joking music videos to show the freedom to express itself as mothers given by the new breast pump.
Four real mothers dance in a barn of cows on a track that includes songs like, “Yes, I’m throwing milk but I don’t have a thing” and “If I didn’t notice it, these are not breasts but my boobs.”
The choir continues: “Pull it out, pull it out, feed the children, pull it out, pull it out, pull the milk to my ladies.”
However, advertising, posted on the company's Facebook pages, caused controversy on the net.
With 77,000 views and hundreds of comments, the video elicited different reactions among the viewers, many of which claimed it highlighted the “horrors” of the dairy industry.
“An unhappy decision to use cows to advertise the product.
Like us, they also have to become pregnant and give birth to produce milk, with the exception of the fact that their children are stolen from them a few days from birth," someone wrote.
Elvie breast pump is worn discreetly under a bra for mothers
Someone else commented: “Understandably traumatic both for the mother and for the child.
But yes, why not use them to advertise a breast pump for moms who will continue to keep their babies?”
Someone else added: "It's really meaningless advertising."
Others defended it and a woman even admitted to finding the song "funny."
“I think it’s a brilliant idea.
If I'm still breastfeeding, I'd like to have one.
When I was using breast pump, I felt just like a cow.
Advertising is a bit irreverent but it doesn't get far from reality.
This is a brilliant product," someone wrote.
Another person commented: "It's a fun advertisement dedicated to moms who use breast pump (often in the workplace or in the bathroom) and they feel like "cows.""
Its purpose is not to commend or judge the dairy industry.”
In the end the protagonists of the video show that they danced with the discrete breast pump tucked into the bra.
The concept to which the campaign is inspired is based on the idea that many women who use breast pumps feel like cows.
The Elvie breast pump is however completely silent, has no threads or tubes and can be tucked into a breastfeeding bra giving women the freedom to move, hold children in their arms and even go out as they pull milk.
Ana Balarin, partner and ECD of Mother commented: "Elvie breastbread is such a revolutionary product that it deserved a bold and provocative launch.
Comparing the women who use breast pump to the cows we wanted to highlight all the challenges of this practice by demonstrating in a fun and easily recognizable way the incredible sense of freedom that the new breast pump is able to ensure.”
This isn't the first time Elvie's made headlines.
During London Fashion Week, the mother of two children showed up on the catwalk of stylist Marta Jakubowski wearing this product.
Hundreds of migrant children quietly moved into a tendopolis on the borders of Texas
The number of migrant children has increased exponentially even though monthly border crossings have remained relatively unchanged, in part because the harsh rhetoric and political line of the Trump administration have made it more difficult to entrust these children.
Most of those who took them into foster care were typically illegal immigrants who are now afraid of jeopardizing the chance of staying in the country offering to make such a gesture.
The risk increased in June when federal authorities announced that potential foster carers and other adult members of their households would be fingerprinted and that the data would be transmitted to immigration authorities.
Last week Matthew Albence, a senior official at the Immigration and Customs Enforcement, said before Congress that the agency arrested dozens of people who had offered to take single children into foster care.
Later the agency later confirmed that 70% of those arrested had no criminal record.
"Almost 80% of people who offer to take children into custody or belong to the families of these people are in the country illegally and most of them are criminal foreigners.
We are therefore continuing to hunt down these people,” said Albence.
To try to prosecute children more quickly, officials have introduced new rules whereby some of them will have to appear in court within a month's time, rather than 60 days, which was the previous standard period, according to those who work at the reception points.
Many will appear by videoconference instead of in person, to plead their lawsuit of legal status with an immigration judge.
Those that will be considered unsuitable to receive help will be expelled quickly.
The longer children remain in foster care, the more they risk suffering from anxiety and depression that cause violent bouts of rabies or escape attempts, according to those who work at the reception points and the relationships that have emerged from the system in recent months.
Those who support this position state that the probability that this will occur is even higher in a large structure such as Tornillo where, precisely because of its size, it is easier for children’s signs of inconvenience to be ignored.
They add that moving children into the tent cities without giving them time to prepare emotionally or greet friends could aggravate the trauma that many are already facing.
