Diplomats noted that the Dominican Republic and Panama have already recognized Beijing, with little resistance from Washington.
In June of 2017 a rather cordial meeting was held between the president of Panama Juan Carlos Varela and President Trump who also had a hotel in Panama until the partners forced the Trump Organization leadership team to leave.
State Department officials have decided to re-enter the heads of U.S. Diplomatic Missions from El Salvador, Dominican Republic and Panama following the "recent decision to no longer recognize Taiwan," department spokeswoman Heather Nauert said in a statement released earlier in the month.
But the decision to apply sanctions was taken only against El Salvador, which in 2017 received from the Americans about 140 million dollars of aid for the control of drug trafficking, development and economic support.
Proposed sanctions, which included cuts to financial aid and visa-giving restrictions, would have been troubling for the Central American country with a high percentage of unemployed and homicides.
After a series of internal meetings, officials from North and Central America postponed a high-level conference focused on security and economic prosperity to hold a similar meeting last year that was seen as a step forward in an attempt to prevent migrants from heading to the United States.
But in mid-September senior management officials made it clear that they intended to continue with the conference thus definitively waiving any sanctions against El Salvador.
Vice President Mike Pence has been tasked with presiding over the conference, now scheduled for mid-October, demonstrating the importance the administration attaches to this meeting, diplomats say.
And the three U.S. envoys returned quietly to El Salvador, Panama and the Dominican Republic without receiving further messages of reproach or punishment from Washington.
A Bolton spokesman for the White House declined to comment on the details of the debate described by the three American officials and two diplomats who agreed to speak, anonymously, about internal decisions.
What they said was confirmed by an external analyst close to the administration who still wanted to maintain anonymity.
Study History
In the report he will publish, Special Counsel Robert Mueller could accuse Trump of obstruction of justice, of which sufficient evidence now seems to exist.
According to what is said, Mueller's investigation intends to shed shed light on Trump's possible collusion with Russia during the presidential campaign.
If Congress goes to the Democrats, Trump would have to respond to these accusations before voters and perhaps to a jury of his peers just as he prepares for a new candidacy.
The unknowns are many and I don't mean to claim that Trump's downfall is inevitable, as is not that of his counterparts in Europe.
On both sides of the Atlantic we will have to make choices that will determine how long the struggle could last.
In 1938, German officers were prepared to organize a coup against Hitler if the Western powers stopped him and supported the Czechoslovaks in Munich.
On that occasion we failed to miss the opportunity to avoid the years of carnage that followed.
The course of history revolves around these turning points where the inexorable march of democracy is accelerated or delayed.
Americans currently have to deal with several.
What would happen if Trump decides to dismiss substitute Attorney General Rod Rosenstein, the man who controls the fate of Mueller's investigation?
Rosenstein's position has become complicated since precisely this newspaper signaled that last year he secretly suggested subjecting Trump to wiretapping by questioning his ability to serve as president.
Rosenstein argues that the news release in The Times newspaper is not true.
"How will we react if Brett Kavanaugh's new FBI investigation will not be complete or impartial or whether it will be confirmed to the Supreme Court despite reliable allegations of attempted rape and perjury?
And above all, in the mid-term elections will we vote for a Congress that will ask Trump to answer for his actions?
If we don't pass these tests, democracy will have to get ready to face a long winter.
But the lesson I learned in Prague makes me think we'll make it.
My mother was a Czechoslovak Jew who was deported to Auschwitz by the same Nazi regime that once occupied my embassy.
She survived, moved to America and, 60 years later, sent me to light the candles of the Sabbath with the swastika.
“With such a legacy, I can only be optimistic about the future.”
Norman Eisen, Senior Fellow at the Brookings Institution, is the president of Washington's Citizens for Responsibility and Ethics organization and author of "The Last Palace: Europe's Turbulent Century in Five Lives and One Legendary House."
