Women are concerned about the costs of long-term care
Even if those who participated in the survey have clear ideas about the type of assistance they would like to receive, few have addressed the topic in the family.
Approximately half of respondents from Nationwide say they have talked to their spouse about the cost of long-term care.
Only 10 percent say they've talked about it with their children.
According to Holly Snyder, vice president of the insurance industry about Nationwide's life, "people want it to be a member of their family to take care of them, but they don't address this conversation."
That's where to start.
Talk about it with your spouse and children: you cannot prepare your family to assist you if you do not communicate in time what you want.
If you have a consultant, decide with him and your family where and how you want to receive assistance, because these factors can be determinantive for costs.
Involve a financial advisor: he can advise you how to pay the expenses that you will face.
The choice of long-term assistance loaning may include a traditional insurance policy or a life-ransomed hybrid policy to cover these expenses or continue to pay insurance money as long as you are able to do so.
Prepare all necessary documents to avoid legal battles for your death.
Appoint a health care attorney so that a person you trust can supervise medical care and make sure professionals meet your wishes in case you are unable to communicate.
Also consider the possibility of a power of attorney for your finances.
You can choose a trusted person who makes financial decisions for you and make sure that your accounts are paid if you are unable.
Don’t forget the details: imagine that your elderly parent is rushed to the hospital.
Would you be able to answer questions about medicines and allergies?
Write this information in detail so that you are always ready.
"It's not just a question of finances, but who are the doctors?" asks Martin.
"Which are medicines?
Who will take care of the dog?
Be prepared for any eventuality.”
Man hit several times with an air rifle in Ilfracombe
A man was hit several times with an air gun as he came home on foot after spending the evening out.
The victim, a forty-year-old, was located in the Oxford Grove area in Ilfracombe, Devon, when he was shot in the chest, abdomen and hand.
Police officers described the shooting, which happened around 02:30 BST, as "random action."
Victim didn't see his attacker.
