Earthquakes & Tsunamis in Indonesia
According to official sources, at least 384 people have died as a result of a very strong earthquake and a tansum that on Friday struck the Indonesian city of Palu. The death toll is set to rise.
The communications are halted and those responsible for the rescue have failed to have any information from the Donggala regency, an area north of Palu that is close to the epicenter of the 7.5-magnitude earthquake.
After the disaster, more than 16,000 people were evacuated from Palu.
Here are some essential data about Palu and Donggala on the island of Sulawesi:
Palu is the capital of Central Sulawesi province, located at the end of a narrow bay on the west coast of Sulawesi Island and with a population of about 379,800 inhabitants in 2017.
When it was hit by the earthquake and tsunami, the city was celebrating its 40th anniversary.
Donggala is a regency that stretches more than 300 km (180 miles) of coastline in the north-west of Sulawesi Island.
The regency, an administrative region dependent on a province, had about 299,200 inhabitants in 2017.
Fishing and agriculture are the main sources of livelihood of the economy of the Central Sulawesi province and especially of the Donggala coastal region.
Nickel extraction is also an important activity in the province, but is concentrated mostly in Morowali, on the opposite coast of Sulawesi.
Palu and Donggala have been hit several times by tsunamis over the past 100 years, according to Indonesia's Disaster Mitigation Agency.
In 1938, a tsunami killed more than 200 people and destroyed hundreds of homes in Donggala.
Another tsunami hit the western Donggala zone in 1996 killing nine people.
Indonesia is located in the seismic area called the Pacific Fire Belt and is often hit by earthquakes.
Here are some of the strongest earthquakes and tsunamis in recent years:
2004: On December 26, a very strong earthquake on the west coast of Aceh province north of Sumatra resulted in a tsunami that struck 14 countries killing, along the coast of the Indian Ocean, 226,000 people, including more than half of Aceh.
2005: A series of strong earthquakes hit the west coast of Sumatra between the end of March and the beginning of April.
Hundreds of people have died on Nias island off the Sumatra coast.
2006: A 6.8-magnitude earthquake struck the south of Java, Indonesia's most populous island, causing a tsunami that lashed out against the south coast killing nearly 700 people.
2009: A 7.6-magnitude quake fell near Padang, the capital of the Western Sumatran province.
There were more than 1,100 victims.
2010: A 7.5-magnitude quake struck one of the Mentawai Islands, off Sumatra, resulting in a 10-metre tsunami that destroyed dozens of villages and killed about 300 people.
2016: A slight earthquake struck the Pidie Jaya regency in Aceh causing destruction and panic in people reminiscent of the devastation of the 2004 earthquake and tsunami.
In this case there was no tsunami but more than 100 people died under the rubble.
2018: A strong earthquake struck the tourist island of Lombok in Indonesia killing more than 500 people, most of them in the northern part of the island.
The quake destroyed thousands of buildings and temporarily stalled thousands of tourists.
Sarah Palin's eldest son has been arrested on suspicion of domestic violence
Track Palin, the eldest son of former Alaska governor and vice presidential candidate Sarah Palin, was arrested on suspicion of assault.
