Federal prosecutors call for extraordinary capital punishment for suspects of terrorist attacks in NYC
Federal prosecutors in New York are calling for capital punishment for Sayfullo Saipov, author of the terrorist attack that left eight people dead: a rare penalty that since 1953 has never been imposed for a federal crime.
Saipov, 30, is accused of using a Home Depot rental van to swoop on a bike lane along the West Side Highway in the Lower Manhattan area hitting pedestrians and cyclists in October.
Under the conditions for applying for capital punishment in the Southern District of New York, to justify the death sentence, prosecutors will have to prove that Saipov "intentionally" killed the eight victims and equally "intentionally" inflicted serious bodily injury.
According to the tribunal document, both of these charges can lead to the death sentence.
Weeks after the attack, a federal grand jury issued 22 charges against Saipov that included eight charges of murder with criminal association, typically used by federal prosecutors in organized crime cases, and an accusation of violence and destruction of motor vehicles.
The attack required "real planning and premeditation," prosecutors claimed describing how Saipov carried it out as "atrocious, cruel and depraved."
"Sayfullo Habibullaevic Saipov caused damage, offenses and losses to the families and friends of Diego Enrique Angelini, Nicholas Cleves, Ann-Laure Decadt, Darren Drake, Ariel Erlij, Hernan Ferruchi, Hernan Diego Mendoza and Alejandro Damian Pagnucco."
Five of the victims were Argentine tourists.
It's been ten years since New York's Southern District prosecuted the last case of capital punishment.
The man sued, Khalid Barnes, was charged with the murder of two drug traffickers but was eventually sentenced to life imprisonment in September 2009.
The death sentence was last carried out in New York in 1953 for the federal case of Julius and Ethel Rosenberg, husband and wife accused of conspiracy through espionage and indicted for passing information to the Soviet Union during the Cold War two years earlier.
Rosenberg both died in an electric chair on June 19, 1953.
According to court documents, Saipov, a native of Uzbekistan, showed no sign of repentance in the days and months following the attack.
Polices say that in front of investigators he was proud of what he did.
According to the indictment, Saipov told authorities that he had decided to carry out the attack after watching ISIS videos on his phone.
He also asked the police to hang the ISIS flag in his hospital room.
With regard to the 22 charges, he pleaded not guilty.
David Patton, one of the federal public defenders representing Saipov, said "obviously disappointed" by the prosecution's decision.
“We believe that the decision to apply for the death penalty instead of accepting recognition of culpability and a life sentence without any possibility of release will only prolong the trauma of these events for everyone involved,” Patton said.
Saipov's group of defenders had previously begged prosecutors not to seek the death penalty.
Tory parliament member says NIGEL FARAGE must conduct Brexit negotiations
Nigel Farage today sworn to "mobilize the people's army" during a protest at the Tory conference.
