Elite League: Dundee Stars 5-3 Belfast Giants
Patrick Dwyer has scored two goals for the Giants against Dundee
The Dundee Stars recovered after Friday's loss in the Elite League game against the Belfast Giants by winning the 5-3 come-back match on Saturday in Dundee.
The Giants immediately advanced by two goals on shots by Patrick Dwyer and Francis Beauvillier.
Mike Sullivan and Jordan Cownie took the home team to the draw before Dwyer brought the Giants back ahead.
Francois Bouchard scored the draw for the Dundees before two Lukas Lundvald Nielsen goals decreed their victory.
It was the third defeat of the Elite League season for Adam Keefe's men who on Friday night in Belfast had reassembled and won the match with the Dundees 2-1.
The two teams have already met four times this season and the Giants have won their first three games.
Dwyer scored the first goal in the 3:35 minute on assist from Kendall McFaull, while Beauvillier doubled after four minutes on David Rutherford's assists.
In what was quite-animated first half, Sullivan brought the home team back into the game at the 13:10 minute before Matt Marquardt provided Cownie's draw assist at the 15:16 minute.
Dwyer decreed the lead for the Giants by scoring the night's second goal at the end of the first half.
The home team regrouped and Bouchard regained the draw after a strategic manoeuvre at the 27:37 minute.
Cownie and Charles Corcoran contributed to Nielsen's action that brought the Dundees ahead for the first time in the match at the end of the second-half and decreed his team's victory by scoring the fifth goal in the middle of the last time.
The Giants, who at this point have lost four of their last five matches, will play their next home game at Milton Keynes on Friday.
Flight controller dies to allow hundreds of passengers to escape the quake
In Indonesia a flight controller will be cheered as a hero after diing to allow a plane carrying hundreds of people to take off putting them to safety.
More than 800 people have died and many are missing due to a very strong earthquake that on Friday struck the island of Sulawesi causing a tsunami.
Severe aftershocks continue to affect the area and many are trapped among the rubble in the city of Palu.
But although his colleagues had fled to save their lives, 21-year-old Anthonius Gunawan Agung remained at his place in the control tower of Palu's Mutiara Sis Al Jufri airport, which fluctuated fearfully.
He wanted to make sure flight Batik Air 6321, which was on the runway, managed to take off without danger.
He then jumped off the control tower when he thought he was about to collapse.
He died later at the hospital.
Air Navigation Indonesia spokesman Yohannes Sirait said the decision may have saved hundreds of lives, Australian broadcaster ABC News reported.
We prepared a helicopter from Balikpapan in Kalimantan to transport him to a larger hospital in another city.
Unfortunately he died this morning before the helicopter reached Palu.
"For us it's really sad news," he added.
Meanwhile, the authorities fear that the number of victims could reach the thousands and the National Agency for Disaster Countermeasures says access to the cities of Donggala, Sigi and Boutong is limited.
"The calculation of the dead is set to increase because many bodies are still under the rubble and it was impossible to reach many others," explains Sutopo agency spokesman Purwo Nugroho.
Waves up to 6 meters high have devastated Palu, where a mass burial will be carried out on Sunday.
Military and commercial aircraft are transporting aid and food.
Risa Kusuma, a 35-year-old mum, told Sky News: "Every minute an ambulance carries bodies.
Clean water is scarce.
Mini-markets are looted everywhere.”
Jan Gelfand, head of the International Red Cross in Indonesia, told CNN: "The Indonesian Red Cross is doing everything possible to help survivors but we don't know what they'll find.
"The situation is already tragic, but it could be much worse."
Indonesia's president, Joko Widodo, arrived in Palu on Sunday and told the men of the country's army: "I ask all of you to work day and night to carry out all activities related to the evacuation.
Are you ready," CNN reported.
During the year Indonesia had already been hit by earthquakes in Lombok, where 550 people had died.
