Analyst reconstructs the night of woman burned alive
On Saturday came the presentation of the prosecution's evidence in the retrial against a man accused of burning a Mississippi woman alive in 2014.
U.S. Department of Justice analyst Paul Rowlett testified for hours as an expert in the field of intelligence analysis.
He explained to the jury how he used cellphone data to reconstruct the movements of the 29-year-old accused Quinton Tellis and nineteen-year-old victim Jessica Chambers, on the night she died.
According to The Clarion Ledger, Rowlett said he had retrieved location data from several cellphones showing that, contrary to what he said, Tellis was with Chambers the night she was killed.
When data showed that his cellphone was with Chambers around the time he claims to have been with friend Michael Sanford, police spoke to Sanford.
Sanford showed up in court on Saturday and testified he wasn't in town that day.
When the prosecutor asked him if Tellis was telling the truth by statinging that night he was in the Sanford truck, the latter confirmed that he was "lying because my truck was in Nashville."
Another inconsistency is that Tellis claimed to have known Chambers for two weeks when she died.
Cell phone data indicate that they had only known each other for a week.
