Two women whose loved ones have committed suicide to prevent others from performing the same gesture.
Sharon Davis and Kelly Humphreys attacked on a bridge in Wales tickets with motivational messages and phone numbers that people can call to receive support.
Davi's son, Tyler, was 13 when he began to suffer from depression and killed himself at 18.
"I don't want any parent to feel the way I feel every day," he says.
Davis, who is 45 and lives in Lydney, recounts that her son was a promising chef with an infectious smile.
“Everyone knew him for his smile.
They always said that he would light up every room he came into."
But before he died he quit his job because depression had taken him "to a really dark place."
In 2014, Tyler's brother, who was then 11, found him after he took his own life.
Davis says, "I'm continuing to fear a ripple effect will occur."
He created tickets "to let people know that there's someone they can turn to to talk about, even if it's a friend.
