North Korea's number of political refugees 'decreased' with Kim
A South Korean legislature says the number of North Koreans demanding political refuge from South Korea has declined since Kim Jong-un came to power seven years ago.
Park Byeong-seug, citing data from the South Korean unification ministry, says refugees were 1,127 last year versus 2,706 in 2011.
Mr Park explains that the main motives are stricter control of the border between North Korea and China and the higher tariffs demanded by those who illegally leave these people.
Pyongyang made no public statements.
The vast majority of North Korea's refugees are eventually offered South Korean citizenship.
According to Seoul, more than 30,000 North Koreans have illegally crossed the border since the end of the Korean War in 1953.
Most escape through China, whose border with North Korea is longer and easier to cross than the heavily protected demilitarized zone (DMZ) between the two Koreas.
China considers them illegal emigrants rather than refugees and often repatriates them by force.
Relations between the North and the South, which are still technically at war, have significantly improved in recent months.
This month the two countries have already met in Pyongyang to talk about denuclearization negotiations that are in a stalemate.
This is after the historic meeting in June between U.S. President Donald Trump and Kim Jong-un in Singapore, in which they agreed in general terms to collaborate to rid the Korean Peninsula of nuclear weapons.
North Korean Foreign Minister Ri Yong-ho on Saturday criticised U.S. sanctions for the lack of developments ever since.
"Without confidence in the U.S. we cannot be certain of our national security and in such circumstances there is no chance that we proceed to unilateral disarmament first," Ri said in a speech at the U.N. General Assembly in New York.
