Minority leader Nancy Pelosi calls Supreme Court nominee Brett Kavanaugh "hysterical" and says she is not temperamentally fit to hold that post.
Pelosi expressed this opinion on Saturday in an interview during the Texas Tribune Festival in Austin, Texas.
"I think that if a woman behaved in the same way, they'd call her hysterical," Pelosi said about the statement made Thursday by Kavanaugh before the Senate Judiciary Committee.
Visibly excitedly Kavanaugh denied the accusation of sexually harassing Professor Christine Blasey Ford when they were both teenagers.
At the beginning of his speech, Kavanaugh was in the throes of emotion, so much so that he almost screamed and with his throat crazed while talking about his family and years in high school.
He also explicitly condemned the committee's Democrats who called the allegations against him an "absurd and coordinated smear campaign" organized by angry liberals because Hillary Clinton lost the 2016 presidential election.
Pelosi believes that Kavanaugh's testimony proves that she cannot enter the Supreme Court because she has proven to be biased against Democrats.
"I think he self-excluded himself with his statements and how he talked about the Clintons and Democrats," he said.
Pelosi then raised an objection when she was asked if she will try to impeach Kavanaugh in case it is confirmed and whether Democrats were to reach a majority in the House of Representatives.
"I think that if you don't speak the truth in Congress or the FBI, it's not only not suitable for the Supreme Court, not even for the tribunal you're presiding over now," Pelosi said.
Kavanaugh is currently a Court of Appeals judge for the District of Columbia Circuit.
Pelosi added that, as a Democrat, she is concerned about Kavanaugh's potential decisions against the Affordable Care Act or the Roe vs. Wade, because he is considered a Conservative judge.
