How could the U.S.-China trade war favor Beijing
The opening blows of the U.S.-China trade war have been thunderous and, while the battle is far from over, experts say a contrast between the two countries could be advantageous for Beijing in the long run.
Donald Trump, the president of the United States, has already issued the first warning this year by taxing China's major exports, including solar panels, steel and aluminum.
The most significant escalation occurred this week with the new tariffs on articles for over 200 billion dollars (175 billion euros) of value. This means that taxes will be applied on half of the goods of Chinese origin.
Beijing has reacted each time with measures of the same type by recently applying tariffs from five to ten percent on 60 billion dollars of American goods.
China has promised to respond to the U.S
For Washington to give up, one would have to give in to his demands, but bending publicly to the U.S. would be too embarrassing for Xi Jinping, the president of China.
Experts, however, believe that if Beijing plays its cards well, the pressures of the trade war with the U.S. can positively affect China in the long term by decreasing the interdependence of the two economies.
"The fact that a swift political decision in Washington or Beijing could create the initial conditions of an economic collapse in both countries is in fact far more dangerous than what has been recognized so far," argues Abigail Grace, a researcher who studies Asia at the Center for New American Security research center.
Syria is "ready" for the return of refugees, the foreign minister argues
Syria says it is ready for the return of refugees and is looking for aid to rebuild the ravaged country from the more than seven years of war.
Facing the U.N. General Assembly, Foreign Minister Walid al-Moualem said the country's conditions are improving.
"Today the situation on the territory is more stable and safe thanks to the progress made in the fight against terrorism," he said.
The government continues to recover areas destroyed by terrorists to restore normality.
Now there are all the conditions for the voluntary return of refugees to the country they have had to leave as a result of terrorism and unilateral economic measures that have affected their daily lives and livelihood.
According to the U.N. estimate, more than 5.5 million Syrians have left the country since the war began in 2011.
Another six million still living in Syria need humanitarian aid.
Al-Moualem has stated that the Syrian regime will gladly accept assistance to rebuild the devastated country.
But he stressed that he would not accept any aid subordinated to conditions or from those countries that supported the insurrection.
Europe clinches victory at Ryder Cup in Paris
The Europa team won the 2018 Ryder Cup beating the USA team with a final score of 17.5 to 10.5 at Le Golf National outside Paris, France.
The U.S. has lost six consecutive times on European soil and hasn’t won a Ryder Cup in Europe since 1993.
Europe regained the trophy when Danish captain Thomas Bjorn's team reached the 14.5 points they needed to beat the United States.
U.S. champion Phil Mickelson, who struggled for most of the tournament, threw his tee shot into the water at the 16th hole of par 3 conceding victory to Francesco Molinari.
The Italian golfer has distinguished himself in all his rounds becoming one of the four players ever to record a 5-0-0 since the tournament with the current formula was first played in 1979.
American Jordan Spieth was defeated with the 5&4 score by the lower Danish player in the European team's Thorbjorn Olesen standings.
The best player in the world, Dustin Johnson, surrendered 2&1 to England's Ian Poulter who may have played his last Ryder Cup.
A Ryder Cup veteran, Spaniard Sergio Garcia who played eight, became the European player who won the most in tournament history with 25.5 points.
"I don't usually cry, but I can't do without it today.
It's been a difficult year.
I'm grateful to Thomas for choosing me and believing in me.
I'm very happy to have regained the cup.
It's a team win and I'm pleased to have made my contribution," declared an excited Garcia after the European victory.
Pass the baton to his compatriot John Ram who beat U.S. golf legend Tiger Woods with the score of 2&1 in Sunday's singles.
"If I think I've grown up watching his meetings, I'm really very proud to have beaten Tiger Woods," the twenty-three-year-old Rahm declared.
Woods has lost all four matches in France and now the count of his Ryder Cup matches amounts to 13 wins, 21 defeats and 3 draws.
A strange statistic if you think he's one of the greatest players of all time having won 14 of the major titles, second only to Jack Nicklaus.
The U.S. team has been trying all weekend to find fairways, with the exception of Patrick Reed, Justin Thomas and Tony Finau, who have played high-level golf throughout the tournament.
U.S. captain Jim Furyk declared after a disappointing performance of his team: "I'm proud of these guys because they've fought.
At one point this morning we put pressure on the Europa team.
We've thrown this opportunity away.
Congratulations to Thomas.
He's a great captain.
All of his 12 players performed very well.
We’re going to reorganize. I’m going to work with the American PGA and the Ryder Cup committee and we’ll move forward.
I'm fond of these 12 guys and I'm proud to be their captain.
We're trying hard.
But they've played better than us."
Red tide update: the concentration decreases in Pinellas, Manatee and Sarasota
The latest Florida Fish and Wildlife Commission report shows a general decrease in red tide concentration in parts of Tampa Bay area.
According to the FWC, patchy flowering conditions, which suggest a decrease in concentration, are evident in areas of Pinellas, Manatee, Sarasota, Charlotte and Collier counties.
Red tide flowering stretches about 130 miles of coastline from northern Pinellas County south of Lee County.
Patches are about 10 miles off Hillsborough County but in fewer areas than last week.
Red tide has also been observed in Pasco County.
Last week an average concentration was reported in Pinellas County or off its shores, a concentration from low to high off Hillsborough County, from an underlying concentration to a high concentration in the County of Manatee, from an underlying concentration to a high concentration in Sarasota County or off its coasts, from an underlying concentration to an average concentration in Charlotte County, from an underlying concentration to a high concentration in County
Cases of respiratory irritation continue to be reported in Pinellas, Manatee, Sarasota, Lee and Collier counties.
While last week no cases were reported in northwest Florida.
